1
00:00:53,796 --> 00:00:55,924
No, son. Never.

2
00:00:56,883 --> 00:00:59,552
The blood stays on the blade.

3
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:04,724
One day you'll understand.

4
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:16,027
Some of it I half remember.

5
00:01:16,569 --> 00:01:18,571
And the rest...

6
00:01:19,155 --> 00:01:21,908
The rest I took from dreams.

7
00:01:25,495 --> 00:01:28,915
St. Michael, the archangel,
defend us in battle.

8
00:01:29,082 --> 00:01:33,670
Be our protector against the snares...

9
00:01:34,170 --> 00:01:37,423
...and the wickedness of the devil.

10
00:01:37,590 --> 00:01:39,425
Now, son, who's that?

11
00:01:39,592 --> 00:01:41,344
- St. Michael.
- Who is it?

12
00:01:41,511 --> 00:01:43,429
- St. Michael!
- And what did he do?

13
00:01:43,596 --> 00:01:46,391
He cast Satan out of paradise.

14
00:01:46,849 --> 00:01:48,726
Good boy.

15
00:02:59,005 --> 00:03:01,883
May God put the steel
of the Holy Spirit in your spine...

16
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,928
...and the love of the Blessed Virgin
in your heart.

17
00:03:31,079 --> 00:03:34,415
- Hey, boyo!
- Johnny.

18
00:03:34,582 --> 00:03:37,502
- What's the battle?
- Natives against the Dead Rabbits.

19
00:03:37,669 --> 00:03:41,339
- Which are you?
- What do you think? Dead Rabbits.

20
00:04:21,462 --> 00:04:23,840
Well, well, Monk.

21
00:04:24,090 --> 00:04:26,092
Are you with us or not?

22
00:04:26,259 --> 00:04:29,470
For the last time, Vallon,
I'm with you if the money is right.

23
00:04:30,471 --> 00:04:33,433
I'll give you 10 per notch.

24
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:37,270
- Ten?
- You have my word.

25
00:04:41,482 --> 00:04:45,278
- Ten per notch?
- Per new notch.

26
00:04:48,990 --> 00:04:50,825
Then I'm your man.

27
00:06:39,684 --> 00:06:42,520
Is this it, Priest?
The pope's new army?

28
00:06:42,687 --> 00:06:46,065
A few crusty bitches
and a handful of ragtags?

29
00:06:46,232 --> 00:06:49,777
Now, now, Bill, you swore this was
a battle between warriors...

30
00:06:49,944 --> 00:06:52,280
...not a bunch of Miss Nancys.

31
00:06:52,447 --> 00:06:55,283
So warriors is what I brought.

32
00:06:58,203 --> 00:06:59,621
The O'Connell Guard.

33
00:06:59,787 --> 00:07:01,289
The Plug Uglies.

34
00:07:01,456 --> 00:07:03,917
The Shirt Tails.

35
00:07:08,796 --> 00:07:10,465
The Chichesters.

36
00:07:10,632 --> 00:07:13,426
The forty Thieves.

37
00:07:29,609 --> 00:07:32,278
On my challenge...

38
00:07:32,487 --> 00:07:38,243
...by the ancient laws of combat,
we have met at this chosen ground...

39
00:07:38,409 --> 00:07:40,828
...to settle, for good and all...

40
00:07:40,995 --> 00:07:44,958
...who holds sway over the Five Points:

41
00:07:45,291 --> 00:07:51,005
Us Natives, born right-wise
to this fine land...

42
00:07:51,172 --> 00:07:54,008
...or the foreign hordes defiling it.

43
00:07:58,555 --> 00:08:03,726
By the ancient laws of combat, I accept
the challenge of the so-called Natives.

44
00:08:03,893 --> 00:08:06,312
You plague our people at every turn.

45
00:08:06,479 --> 00:08:09,357
But from this day out,
you shall plague us no more.

46
00:08:09,524 --> 00:08:13,319
For let it be known that the hand
that tries to strike us from this land...

47
00:08:13,486 --> 00:08:15,196
...shall be swiftly cut down.

48
00:08:17,365 --> 00:08:20,827
Then may the Christian Lord
guide my hand...

49
00:08:20,994 --> 00:08:23,246
...against your Roman popery!

50
00:08:23,413 --> 00:08:26,207
Prepare to receive the true Lord!

51
00:10:29,247 --> 00:10:31,165
Priest!

52
00:11:19,214 --> 00:11:21,633
Look to me.

53
00:11:22,967 --> 00:11:26,054
Who is this under my knife?

54
00:11:34,062 --> 00:11:36,564
Father, get up. Get up!

55
00:11:36,731 --> 00:11:40,318
Where are you? Where are you?

56
00:11:41,653 --> 00:11:43,196
Oh, my son.

57
00:11:46,491 --> 00:11:50,161
Don't never look away.

58
00:11:57,335 --> 00:12:00,004
Soon be over, Priest.

59
00:12:02,006 --> 00:12:04,008
Finish it.

60
00:12:22,026 --> 00:12:24,946
You may need this across the river.

61
00:12:33,872 --> 00:12:37,292
Ears and noses will be
the trophies of the day.

62
00:12:38,126 --> 00:12:41,963
But no hand shall touch him!

63
00:12:44,048 --> 00:12:46,885
No hand shall touch him!

64
00:12:48,928 --> 00:12:51,556
He'll cross over whole...

65
00:12:52,557 --> 00:12:54,267
...in honor.

66
00:12:54,434 --> 00:12:57,145
Not before I get what's owed.

67
00:13:03,776 --> 00:13:05,778
No!

68
00:13:06,821 --> 00:13:11,326
Lt's fair. A touch indelicate, but fair.

69
00:13:15,955 --> 00:13:17,957
My sympathies.

70
00:13:18,875 --> 00:13:20,501
What will we do with the boy?

71
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:23,129
Look at me.

72
00:13:28,801 --> 00:13:30,470
Give him to the law.

73
00:13:30,637 --> 00:13:33,348
See he gets a good education.

74
00:13:34,015 --> 00:13:36,976
Okay, boy, say goodbye to your father.

75
00:13:44,025 --> 00:13:46,236
- Get him!
- Get him!

76
00:13:47,153 --> 00:13:48,655
Don't let him get away.

77
00:13:48,821 --> 00:13:51,324
Hurry, come in here!

78
00:13:53,493 --> 00:13:55,119
He's going downstairs.

79
00:14:11,511 --> 00:14:12,762
Come here, boy.

80
00:14:12,929 --> 00:14:15,848
- You're going to Hellgate, son.
- There he is. Get him.

81
00:14:16,015 --> 00:14:19,018
- Priest Vallon died a noble death...
- Let go of me!

82
00:14:19,185 --> 00:14:24,607
...but his Dead Rabbits
is done and outlawed!

83
00:14:26,359 --> 00:14:31,447
Let no one even speak their name
from this time on.

84
00:15:16,117 --> 00:15:21,623
In this place, you have grown
from a boy into a man.

85
00:15:23,458 --> 00:15:27,170
Put to death the earthly things in you:
Immorality, impurity...

86
00:15:27,337 --> 00:15:29,964
...passion, vengeance.

87
00:15:30,131 --> 00:15:33,468
The Lord has forgiven you.

88
00:15:33,635 --> 00:15:36,971
You also must forgive.

89
00:15:37,764 --> 00:15:40,433
You go forth to a country torn apart
by civil strife.

90
00:15:42,810 --> 00:15:44,812
Thank you, Reverend.

91
00:15:47,857 --> 00:15:50,652
Lend your hand to the work
that yet remains...

92
00:15:50,818 --> 00:15:53,029
...that this war may end...

93
00:15:53,196 --> 00:15:56,574
...and the plague of slavery
that brought this conflagration...

94
00:15:56,741 --> 00:16:02,163
...down upon us vanish forever
from the earth.

95
00:16:32,777 --> 00:16:34,696
In the second year of the Civil War...

96
00:16:34,862 --> 00:16:37,448
...when the Irish Brigade
marched in the streets...

97
00:16:37,615 --> 00:16:40,660
...New York was a city full of tribes...

98
00:16:40,827 --> 00:16:44,205
...war chiefs, rich and poor.

99
00:16:44,372 --> 00:16:48,167
- Down with Lincoln!
- Lincoln will make all white men slaves.

100
00:16:48,543 --> 00:16:50,044
It wasn 't a city really.

101
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:55,717
It was more a furnace where a city
someday might be forged.

102
00:16:55,884 --> 00:16:57,552
That's the spirit, boys.

103
00:16:57,719 --> 00:17:02,056
- Go off and die for your blackie friends.
- Down with the abolitionists!

104
00:17:02,223 --> 00:17:05,393
We shoul�ve run a better man against
Lincoln when we had the chance.

105
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:07,395
New York should secede
from the Union!

106
00:17:07,562 --> 00:17:10,315
They trying to say we're
no different than niggers?

107
00:17:10,481 --> 00:17:13,401
You ain't. Good evening, brother.

108
00:17:13,943 --> 00:17:15,820
- Praise the Union.
- The Union forever!

109
00:17:15,987 --> 00:17:18,823
Go back to Africa, nigger!

110
00:17:19,282 --> 00:17:22,243
- Go back to Africa!
- Leave him alone. We're free.

111
00:17:22,410 --> 00:17:25,496
The angriest talk was of the
new Conscription Act...

112
00:17:25,663 --> 00:17:27,498
...the first draft in Union history.

113
00:17:40,511 --> 00:17:44,265
Join the Army, lads. Three square meals
a day and good pay in your pocket.

114
00:17:44,432 --> 00:17:47,852
When the Irish came,
the city was in a fever.

115
00:17:48,019 --> 00:17:52,607
Since the time of the great famine,
they'd come streaming off the boats.

116
00:17:52,774 --> 00:17:54,525
And they got a right warm welcome.

117
00:17:54,692 --> 00:17:58,112
- Go back to Ireland, you dumb micks!
- Remember that, you Irish gyps!

118
00:17:58,279 --> 00:18:00,198
Get back on the boat, Paddy!

119
00:18:00,865 --> 00:18:04,077
I only came two hours downriver
from Hellgate.

120
00:18:04,244 --> 00:18:06,621
But they all took me for an immigrant.

121
00:18:06,788 --> 00:18:09,874
Why not? There were a thousand
different accents in New York.

122
00:18:10,041 --> 00:18:14,754
And to the Natives, you see,
we was all the same.

123
00:18:19,926 --> 00:18:23,429
Welcome to America, son.
Your long, arduous journey is over.

124
00:18:23,596 --> 00:18:25,974
- Go back to your own country!
- Vote Tammany!

125
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:37,986
New York loved William Tweed
and hated him.

126
00:18:38,152 --> 00:18:42,448
And those of us trying to be thieves,
well, we couldn 't help but admire him.

127
00:18:42,824 --> 00:18:46,119
Mr. Cutting, gentlemen,
thank you for coming. Lt's an honor.

128
00:18:46,286 --> 00:18:48,162
Mr. Tweed.

129
00:18:50,248 --> 00:18:52,500
Sir, please. Excuse me.

130
00:18:52,834 --> 00:18:55,086
- I think you're frightening them.
- So?

131
00:18:55,253 --> 00:18:59,007
Don't mind him.
He used to be an Irishman.

132
00:19:00,466 --> 00:19:02,176
Ah, the Five Points.

133
00:19:02,468 --> 00:19:07,557
Murderers ' Alley. Brickbat Mansion.
The Gates of Hell.

134
00:19:07,724 --> 00:19:13,605
Here in this vile place.
Look upon the face of this poor child.

135
00:19:13,771 --> 00:19:16,399
She lives in squalor...

136
00:19:16,566 --> 00:19:20,945
...in this godforsaken den
of vice and misery behind me.

137
00:19:21,404 --> 00:19:23,823
Every year the reformers came.

138
00:19:23,990 --> 00:19:25,700
Every year the Points got worse...

139
00:19:28,036 --> 00:19:29,871
...as if it liked being dirty.

140
00:19:30,747 --> 00:19:34,083
- Where am I going to go?
- The Reverend wants you out of here.

141
00:19:34,250 --> 00:19:36,502
You can't do this!

142
00:19:38,254 --> 00:19:41,716
A glorious resurrection will spring
from the filthy depths...

143
00:19:41,883 --> 00:19:45,345
...to which these miserable creatures
have fallen.

144
00:19:45,511 --> 00:19:48,223
In God, they will find their true home.

145
00:19:48,389 --> 00:19:52,018
You may or may not know, Bill,
that every day I go to the waterfront...

146
00:19:52,185 --> 00:19:54,854
...with hot soup for the Irish
as they come ashore.

147
00:19:55,021 --> 00:19:57,106
Lt's part of building a political base.

148
00:19:57,273 --> 00:20:00,193
I've noticed you there.
You may have noticed me.

149
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:03,613
Indeed I have. Throwing torrents
of pavers and withering abuse...

150
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:07,367
...on everyone stepping off those boats.
- Lf only I had the guns...

151
00:20:07,534 --> 00:20:12,372
...I'd shoot each and every one of them
before they set foot on American soil.

152
00:20:37,814 --> 00:20:40,316
Mulberry Street...

153
00:20:41,067 --> 00:20:42,819
...and Worth.

154
00:20:42,986 --> 00:20:47,740
Cross and Orange and Little Water.

155
00:20:47,907 --> 00:20:50,827
Each of the Five Points is a finger.

156
00:20:50,994 --> 00:20:55,331
When I close my hand,
it becomes a fist.

157
00:20:55,498 --> 00:20:59,586
And any time that I wish,
I can turn it against you.

158
00:20:59,752 --> 00:21:02,005
I know, but we're talking
about different things.

159
00:21:02,171 --> 00:21:05,633
I'm talking about civic duty,
responsibilities we owe to the people.

160
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:08,678
Schools and hospitals,
sewers and utilities.

161
00:21:08,845 --> 00:21:11,180
Street construction,
repairs and sweeping.

162
00:21:11,347 --> 00:21:13,600
Business licenses, saloon licenses...

163
00:21:34,829 --> 00:21:39,334
St. Michael, the archangel,
defend us in battle.

164
00:21:39,500 --> 00:21:43,338
Be our protector against the snares
and the wickedness of the devil.

165
00:21:43,504 --> 00:21:46,132
Street cars, ferries, rubbish disposal.

166
00:21:46,299 --> 00:21:49,177
There's a power of money
to be made in this city, Bill.

167
00:21:49,344 --> 00:21:53,389
With your help, the people
must be made to understand...

168
00:21:53,556 --> 00:21:57,644
...that all these things are best kept
within the Tammany family...

169
00:21:57,810 --> 00:22:00,021
...which is why I'm proposing
an alliance...

170
00:22:00,188 --> 00:22:02,607
...between our two great organizations.

171
00:22:02,774 --> 00:22:07,111
- You're talking about muscle work.
- That too. Muscle to match our spirit.

172
00:22:07,278 --> 00:22:09,155
You own the crushers.
Get them to do it.

173
00:22:09,322 --> 00:22:11,282
The police? Oh, no. Jesus, no!

174
00:22:11,449 --> 00:22:15,286
The appearance of the law
must be upheld...

175
00:22:15,453 --> 00:22:17,997
...especially while it's being broken.

176
00:22:29,384 --> 00:22:32,887
Give me the strength
for what I must do.

177
00:22:35,890 --> 00:22:37,725
Who are you?

178
00:22:40,228 --> 00:22:42,105
- Hey!
- No!

179
00:22:42,272 --> 00:22:45,525
I said, who are you?
What are you doing here?

180
00:22:53,074 --> 00:22:55,994
I just like it down here is all.

181
00:22:56,744 --> 00:22:59,247
See what's in his pockets, Jimmy.

182
00:22:59,414 --> 00:23:02,917
- Look, boys, I really don't want a fight.
- Don't want a fight?

183
00:23:03,084 --> 00:23:06,337
Don't worry, son,
ain't gonna be much a fight.

184
00:23:13,511 --> 00:23:15,430
Don't kill me.

185
00:23:23,438 --> 00:23:25,440
Where'd you get that?

186
00:23:25,607 --> 00:23:28,026
I told you I didn't want a fight!

187
00:23:28,192 --> 00:23:30,904
Why not? Look how good you done.

188
00:23:38,286 --> 00:23:43,041
- You're the Priest's son, aren't you?
- You, get away from me, understand?

189
00:23:43,208 --> 00:23:47,879
You don't remember me, do you?
I's the one tried to help you.

190
00:23:49,631 --> 00:23:52,634
- What?
- I's the one tried to help you...

191
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:55,136
...when the Natives took you.

192
00:23:58,723 --> 00:24:03,728
- Oh, that was you?
- Yeah. I thought you was killed.

193
00:24:06,439 --> 00:24:08,441
They just locked me up,
you know that?

194
00:24:08,608 --> 00:24:10,610
This long?

195
00:24:10,777 --> 00:24:15,615
Well, I kept trying to escape, you know.
They add on time for that.

196
00:24:16,658 --> 00:24:19,285
So, what are you doing back here?

197
00:24:19,953 --> 00:24:21,996
I guess I missed the place.

198
00:24:28,836 --> 00:24:30,588
Bowery Boys.

199
00:24:43,017 --> 00:24:44,477
Get back here!

200
00:24:44,644 --> 00:24:47,480
The Five Points, Paradise Square.

201
00:24:47,647 --> 00:24:50,692
The streets here are always lively
of an evening.

202
00:24:51,693 --> 00:24:53,403
Who are the gangs around now?

203
00:24:53,570 --> 00:24:55,530
The Daybreak Boys
and the Swamp Angels.

204
00:24:55,697 --> 00:24:57,866
They work the river, looting ships.

205
00:24:58,032 --> 00:25:00,743
The frog Hollows shanghai sailors
by the Bloody Angle.

206
00:25:00,910 --> 00:25:04,414
The Shirt Tails was rough,
but they've become a bunch of dandies...

207
00:25:04,581 --> 00:25:07,500
...Iolling around Murderers' Alley,
looking like Chinamen.

208
00:25:07,750 --> 00:25:10,461
Hell-Cat Maggie, she tried to open
her own grog shop.

209
00:25:10,628 --> 00:25:13,256
She drunk her own liquor
and got throwed on the street.

210
00:25:13,423 --> 00:25:15,967
- Beautiful.
- Now she's on the lay for anything.

211
00:25:16,134 --> 00:25:18,678
The Plug Uglies are from deep
in the old country.

212
00:25:18,845 --> 00:25:21,097
Got their own language
no one understands.

213
00:25:21,264 --> 00:25:24,642
They love to fight the cops.
The Night Walkers of Ragpicker's Row.

214
00:25:25,101 --> 00:25:27,770
They work on their backs
and kill with their hands.

215
00:25:27,937 --> 00:25:31,691
They're so scurvy only the Plug Uglies
talk to them. But who understands them?

216
00:25:31,941 --> 00:25:34,402
The Slaughter Housers
and the Broadway Twisters.

217
00:25:34,569 --> 00:25:37,697
They're a fine bunch of bingo boys.
And the Little forty Thieves.

218
00:25:37,864 --> 00:25:39,282
I used to run with them...

219
00:25:39,449 --> 00:25:42,368
...till they got took over
by Bendrick the Cockroach.

220
00:25:42,535 --> 00:25:44,037
Bendrick carries a germ.

221
00:25:44,204 --> 00:25:47,123
If you try to leave the gang,
he hacks up blood on you.

222
00:25:47,290 --> 00:25:50,710
The lime-juicers are nothing but
a bunch of rapacious grab-ups.

223
00:25:51,461 --> 00:25:53,755
The True Blue Americans
say they're a gang...

224
00:25:53,922 --> 00:25:56,674
...but all they do is stand on corners
damning England.

225
00:25:56,841 --> 00:26:00,136
If you believe one word
the British say, you're a fool.

226
00:26:00,303 --> 00:26:03,640
Do any of them got the sand
of the Dead Rabbits?

227
00:26:07,268 --> 00:26:09,437
You don't say that name.

228
00:26:09,604 --> 00:26:14,275
That name died with your...
They been outlawed.

229
00:26:14,776 --> 00:26:16,778
In the blockhouse,
the Chinks told me...

230
00:26:16,945 --> 00:26:21,282
...that the Natives celebrate their victory
every year. Is that true?

231
00:26:21,783 --> 00:26:24,327
Aye, that they do. Lt's quite the affair.

232
00:26:24,494 --> 00:26:27,372
The Butcher himself's got to invite you,
or you don't go.

233
00:26:37,966 --> 00:26:40,885
Look where you're going, Johnny.

234
00:26:43,638 --> 00:26:46,474
You look stunned and poorly, sir.

235
00:26:50,812 --> 00:26:53,982
Quite a pair of conversationists,
aren't you?

236
00:26:56,192 --> 00:26:59,821
Maybe not, but we're deep thinkers.

237
00:27:03,157 --> 00:27:09,330
Well, gentlemen, I leave you
in the grace and favor of the Lord.

238
00:27:16,421 --> 00:27:19,090
Jenny, the finest bludget
in all the Points.

239
00:27:19,257 --> 00:27:21,843
She is a prim-looking stargazer.

240
00:27:24,429 --> 00:27:26,848
But I'd check my pockets if I was you...

241
00:27:27,015 --> 00:27:30,268
...because I do believe she lifted
your timepiece.

242
00:27:34,188 --> 00:27:36,691
Well, I let her take it.

243
00:27:36,858 --> 00:27:39,611
I let her take things all the time.

244
00:27:40,111 --> 00:27:42,655
- Is that right?
- Yeah.

245
00:27:47,202 --> 00:27:49,621
We always liked a good fire
in the Points.

246
00:27:50,872 --> 00:27:56,544
You could pick up a little swag. And
if the cops came, you really got a show.

247
00:27:56,711 --> 00:28:00,131
The Municipal Police fought
the Metropolitan Police.

248
00:28:00,298 --> 00:28:03,051
The Metropolitan Police,
they fought the street gangs.

249
00:28:03,218 --> 00:28:05,637
Hurry up, men,
before the Black Joke get there!

250
00:28:05,803 --> 00:28:09,557
There were 37 amateur fire brigades,
and they all fought each other.

251
00:28:09,807 --> 00:28:15,647
The Black Joke are on their way, Tweed,
and they'll beat the shite out of you.

252
00:28:17,982 --> 00:28:23,905
Okay, boys, get the hose out!

253
00:28:29,077 --> 00:28:31,663
Lt's the Black Joke!

254
00:28:31,829 --> 00:28:34,249
Go get them, boys!

255
00:28:34,999 --> 00:28:37,377
Give those Bowery Boys hell!

256
00:28:37,544 --> 00:28:41,172
- Quick, before there's nothing left.
- Go back to the Bowery, you bums!

257
00:28:41,339 --> 00:28:44,175
Stop them. For Go�s sake,
they're taking everything!

258
00:28:44,342 --> 00:28:47,095
In your next time of trouble,
call on Tammany first.

259
00:28:47,262 --> 00:28:50,598
But it's not too late.
You can still save my house.

260
00:28:51,266 --> 00:28:52,934
Let's go.

261
00:28:53,685 --> 00:28:56,604
I thought you said you was hungry.

262
00:29:00,024 --> 00:29:03,570
Grab what you can, Johnny.
Let's get out of here.

263
00:29:04,237 --> 00:29:07,115
May I point out that this building
is burning to ashes?

264
00:29:07,282 --> 00:29:11,619
May I point out this area is the province
of my own Americus fire Brigade...

265
00:29:11,786 --> 00:29:15,248
...and that you lot belong
only in the Bowery?

266
00:29:16,416 --> 00:29:19,460
May I point out that you're outmanned,
outmaneuvered...

267
00:29:19,627 --> 00:29:21,754
...and, in a moment, outfought?

268
00:29:21,921 --> 00:29:23,756
Am I?

269
00:29:29,596 --> 00:29:32,765
There's the Black Joke!
Let's take them on the cobbles.

270
00:29:32,932 --> 00:29:37,312
Go spill some claret, boys.
Go on, Shorty, have a nice muss.

271
00:29:37,478 --> 00:29:40,148
Clear the way for Bill the Butcher.

272
00:29:40,315 --> 00:29:42,483
Okay, boys, to work.

273
00:29:43,610 --> 00:29:47,322
What's the point? The fire's near burned
anything of value inside.

274
00:29:47,488 --> 00:29:50,783
Boys, forget that one.
Next building over.

275
00:29:50,950 --> 00:29:53,786
Mustn't let it spread.
Take what you want from that one.

276
00:29:53,953 --> 00:29:54,996
What are you doing?

277
00:29:55,163 --> 00:29:57,957
There's nothing wrong with this one.
This is my house.

278
00:30:01,794 --> 00:30:03,254
- Johnny!
- Help!

279
00:30:03,421 --> 00:30:05,924
- Johnny!
- Help!

280
00:30:07,342 --> 00:30:10,136
Help! Help!

281
00:30:14,098 --> 00:30:15,892
Help!

282
00:30:19,854 --> 00:30:22,023
Come on. Come on, Johnny, get up.

283
00:30:22,482 --> 00:30:24,400
Leave that!

284
00:30:31,699 --> 00:30:33,117
You two, out of the way!

285
00:30:50,510 --> 00:30:52,720
His name's Amsterdam.

286
00:30:55,223 --> 00:30:58,226
- How's the beak?
- Ain't so bad.

287
00:30:58,393 --> 00:31:02,480
You. You got anything,
you give it up or you get out.

288
00:31:13,700 --> 00:31:15,577
Is that it?

289
00:31:15,952 --> 00:31:17,954
That's it.

290
00:31:19,414 --> 00:31:22,750
Right. Here's the rake.
Everything comes here. We fence it.

291
00:31:22,917 --> 00:31:26,379
Johnny takes our tribute to the Natives,
and we chop up the winnings...

292
00:31:26,546 --> 00:31:30,049
...each to his equal portion,
amongst the gang.

293
00:31:30,216 --> 00:31:33,720
Does that meet
with your approval, Hellgate?

294
00:31:33,887 --> 00:31:35,513
My approval?

295
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:38,558
What's the matter, Jack Sprat?
Can't think for yourself?

296
00:31:40,268 --> 00:31:43,229
- I'll slit your fucking throat!
- Hey, come on!

297
00:31:43,396 --> 00:31:44,606
Hey!

298
00:31:48,109 --> 00:31:52,113
You boys will settle with me
before settling with each other.

299
00:31:55,533 --> 00:31:58,286
I come for my due and proper.

300
00:32:17,222 --> 00:32:19,390
Well, this ain't a bad haul.

301
00:32:19,891 --> 00:32:23,436
When folks start in to saying
to where the country is going to hell...

302
00:32:23,603 --> 00:32:26,564
...I always tell them,
"You just look at all the hard work...

303
00:32:26,731 --> 00:32:30,151
...our fine young lads is doing
down in the Five Points."

304
00:32:30,318 --> 00:32:34,364
Oh, yeah. Now this is just
the thing for Mrs. Mulraney.

305
00:32:34,531 --> 00:32:36,866
All right there, Happy Jack,
leave us something...

306
00:32:37,033 --> 00:32:39,410
...to quarter to Bill the Butcher, eh?

307
00:32:39,577 --> 00:32:41,162
Would you?

308
00:32:52,715 --> 00:32:55,343
Not my favorite tune.

309
00:33:02,517 --> 00:33:04,769
Thank you, boys.

310
00:33:05,311 --> 00:33:08,314
You keep out of trouble now.

311
00:33:26,374 --> 00:33:30,503
Every year the Natives celebrated
the killing of my father all over again...

312
00:33:32,130 --> 00:33:37,218
...at Sparrow's Chinese Pagoda
in Mott Street.

313
00:33:37,385 --> 00:33:41,055
The Chinks hated the Natives
worse than we did.

314
00:33:43,892 --> 00:33:48,229
The drum rolls, and the Butcher
drinks a glass of fire.

315
00:33:52,358 --> 00:33:54,777
When you kill a king...

316
00:33:55,320 --> 00:33:58,281
...you don 't stab him in the dark.

317
00:33:59,699 --> 00:34:04,954
You kill him where the whole court
can watch him die.

318
00:34:08,875 --> 00:34:11,419
Hey, you got any timber?

319
00:35:01,386 --> 00:35:05,223
- Hey, Maggie. Right ear or left ear?
- Give us a drink, you idiot.

320
00:35:05,390 --> 00:35:07,016
Help yourself.

321
00:35:07,183 --> 00:35:09,060
- Final bets!
- To health.

322
00:35:09,227 --> 00:35:12,313
Place your bets.
Betting closes in two minutes.

323
00:35:12,480 --> 00:35:13,773
Place your bets.

324
00:35:13,940 --> 00:35:18,111
Gentlemen, the match
is due to commence.

325
00:35:18,278 --> 00:35:21,823
The count to beat is 25 rodents
in three minutes.

326
00:35:21,990 --> 00:35:24,284
Towser against the vermin.

327
00:35:24,450 --> 00:35:26,160
- Are the enumerators satisfied?
- Fifty!

328
00:35:26,327 --> 00:35:28,454
- Yes!
- Let them go!

329
00:35:42,302 --> 00:35:45,430
- Where you going, boy?
- I'm here to pay tribute to Bill.

330
00:35:45,597 --> 00:35:48,016
Are you now? Give it to me.
I'll give it to him.

331
00:35:48,182 --> 00:35:50,977
No, thanks. I'll give it to him myself.

332
00:35:52,604 --> 00:35:55,106
What do you want to keep,
the money or your teeth?

333
00:35:55,273 --> 00:35:56,983
John.

334
00:35:57,859 --> 00:35:59,527
Welcome.

335
00:36:07,368 --> 00:36:10,038
From me and me lads, sir.

336
00:36:16,669 --> 00:36:19,964
Mate of yours? Where's he from?

337
00:36:20,590 --> 00:36:22,884
Oh, he's not from here, sir.

338
00:36:25,553 --> 00:36:27,263
You.

339
00:36:44,572 --> 00:36:46,824
That's close enough.

340
00:36:57,835 --> 00:37:02,423
Your friend can't look me in the eye.
That's not an admirable characteristic.

341
00:37:02,590 --> 00:37:06,511
No one can look you in the eye, Bill.
Not when you're playing cards.

342
00:37:25,113 --> 00:37:28,783
This is whist. Lt's a gentleman's game.
Make a gentleman's bet.

343
00:37:29,158 --> 00:37:33,413
- I'm betting large, Bill.
- That ain't large.

344
00:37:41,880 --> 00:37:45,049
Please, don't make that noise again,
Harvey.

345
00:37:47,468 --> 00:37:50,889
I like a man who's willing
to burn for his swag.

346
00:37:54,267 --> 00:37:56,936
How do you fare on water?
Come closer.

347
00:37:57,103 --> 00:38:00,440
Close, John. I ain't gonna bite. Close.

348
00:38:03,735 --> 00:38:07,822
There's a Portuguese ship lying low
in the harbor, quarantined three weeks.

349
00:38:07,989 --> 00:38:10,158
Get there before
the Daybreak Boys strip her...

350
00:38:10,325 --> 00:38:11,993
...maybe you and me will talk more.

351
00:38:12,160 --> 00:38:14,704
- Consider it done, sir.
- Good boy.

352
00:38:15,830 --> 00:38:19,500
And you, whatever your name is...
What is your name?

353
00:38:23,171 --> 00:38:29,552
- Amsterdam, sir.
- Amsterdam. I'm New York.

354
00:38:29,761 --> 00:38:32,305
Don't you never come in here
empty-handed again.

355
00:38:32,472 --> 00:38:35,850
You gotta pay for the pleasure
of my company.

356
00:38:36,017 --> 00:38:37,936
Take him for a boat ride, John.

357
00:38:38,102 --> 00:38:41,231
Who knows, but he might
save your life again.

358
00:38:44,859 --> 00:38:47,654
The Daybreak Boys catch us,
they'll slit our throats.

359
00:38:47,820 --> 00:38:50,532
I'll do it myself in a minute
if you don't keep quiet.

360
00:38:50,698 --> 00:38:53,868
I never liked the harbor after dark.

361
00:38:54,035 --> 00:38:57,413
And now each night they brung ashore
the bodies of the soldiers.

362
00:38:57,580 --> 00:38:59,499
Many mothers have not
even the solace...

363
00:38:59,666 --> 00:39:02,043
...of knowing where
their sons have fallen.

364
00:39:02,210 --> 00:39:04,921
I lost my own eldest at Antietam.

365
00:39:05,088 --> 00:39:07,715
His mother and I were unable
to recover his remains.

366
00:39:07,882 --> 00:39:10,593
- It was a mournful sight.
- The war can't last forever.

367
00:39:10,760 --> 00:39:13,471
But we had business of our own.

368
00:39:22,188 --> 00:39:27,193
The Daybreak Boys have been here,
and there's nothing left. Let's go back.

369
00:39:30,822 --> 00:39:33,366
What the hell?

370
00:39:40,206 --> 00:39:42,333
Jimmy, get down!

371
00:39:44,544 --> 00:39:48,423
Jesus, that'll bring the harbor cops
for sure. Fuck off.

372
00:39:56,097 --> 00:39:58,224
Let's take a look.

373
00:40:00,935 --> 00:40:03,271
Jesus, they killed everybody.

374
00:40:03,438 --> 00:40:06,274
There's nothing here but a bunch
of dead sea-crabs.

375
00:40:06,441 --> 00:40:08,735
Come on, let's go.

376
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:12,780
- Shove off.
- No, wait for Amsterdam.

377
00:40:12,947 --> 00:40:15,116
Where's Amsterdam?

378
00:40:17,577 --> 00:40:20,121
- No, you damn fool, take him!
- What in the hell for?!

379
00:40:20,288 --> 00:40:22,415
Look in my glims.
I said no less than 15.

380
00:40:22,582 --> 00:40:25,960
- Is this fresh?
- Four hours most.

381
00:40:26,294 --> 00:40:28,880
Much obliged, gents.

382
00:40:35,929 --> 00:40:38,056
What's that word?

383
00:40:38,973 --> 00:40:41,309
- It means body snatchers.
- I didn't ask the meaning.

384
00:40:41,476 --> 00:40:42,810
I asked the word.

385
00:40:43,478 --> 00:40:44,979
Ghoul.

386
00:40:46,523 --> 00:40:49,943
Ghoul? That's a good word.

387
00:40:50,109 --> 00:40:56,449
"Ghoul Gang Slaughters.
A fresh Outrage in the Five Points."

388
00:40:56,616 --> 00:40:59,077
- That's a notice you can be proud of.
- Thank you.

389
00:40:59,244 --> 00:41:02,997
A low thing, to do that to a body.
Low.

390
00:41:03,164 --> 00:41:05,458
Why? They could have left
that ship with nothing.

391
00:41:05,625 --> 00:41:09,837
Instead they made The Police Gazette,
a periodical of note.

392
00:41:10,004 --> 00:41:12,382
A body's supposed to stay
beneath the earth...

393
00:41:12,549 --> 00:41:15,718
...wearing a wooden coat
until the Resurrection.

394
00:41:15,885 --> 00:41:18,846
These two are a pair of sons
of Irish bitches, same as you.

395
00:41:19,013 --> 00:41:21,057
It don't seem to bother them none.

396
00:41:21,224 --> 00:41:24,143
But then maybe they don't share
your religious scruples.

397
00:41:24,310 --> 00:41:27,105
Maybe they're just a couple
of fidlam Bens.

398
00:41:32,694 --> 00:41:36,698
I've been called a lot of things, mister,
but I ain't never been called...

399
00:41:36,865 --> 00:41:40,535
- fidlam Bens.
- Fidlam Bens. Right.

400
00:41:40,702 --> 00:41:47,083
Well, if I knew what in the hell that
meant, I'd be inclined to take offense.

401
00:41:47,250 --> 00:41:51,504
A fidlam Bens is a fellow
who steals anything, dead or alive...

402
00:41:51,671 --> 00:41:54,716
...because he's too low to work up
a decent lay for himself.

403
00:41:54,883 --> 00:41:57,176
Count that careful, Bill.
Count that careful.

404
00:41:57,343 --> 00:42:00,388
- I'm telling you, that's all there is...
- Now, chiseler...

405
00:42:00,555 --> 00:42:04,100
If you'd said "chiseler," now there's
a word I understand. You know?

406
00:42:04,267 --> 00:42:08,396
- Now, is that what you're calling us?
- I can think of many things to call you.

407
00:42:08,563 --> 00:42:11,858
Right. But I asked
if you was calling us chiselers.

408
00:42:12,025 --> 00:42:14,777
Supposing I am?

409
00:42:16,738 --> 00:42:19,407
Well, then we got business.

410
00:42:20,241 --> 00:42:22,619
That we do.

411
00:42:29,417 --> 00:42:32,378
- A fight! There's a fight!
- Two bucks on McGloin.

412
00:42:32,545 --> 00:42:35,006
- Four bits on the kid.
- Five bits on the kid.

413
00:42:35,173 --> 00:42:38,051
I got two on Amsterdam.

414
00:42:46,517 --> 00:42:49,938
Come on, McGloin, he's just a kid.

415
00:42:50,522 --> 00:42:53,107
Four bits to back Amsterdam.

416
00:42:59,781 --> 00:43:02,700
Watch his left, McGloin. Watch his left.

417
00:43:42,240 --> 00:43:45,451
- All right, that'll do. That'll do.
- Anything to say now, huh?!

418
00:43:45,618 --> 00:43:48,246
That'll do, for chrissake.
Drag him off.

419
00:43:48,413 --> 00:43:51,583
- That's enough, kid.
- You got him. Come on, kid, you won.

420
00:43:51,749 --> 00:43:54,586
McGloin. He's a kid too, McGloin.

421
00:43:54,752 --> 00:43:57,338
You're getting too old for it, McGloin.

422
00:44:18,902 --> 00:44:24,365
McGloin, how would that head look
without the ears and the nose on it?

423
00:44:25,617 --> 00:44:28,036
You'd better leave
that head alone, Bill.

424
00:44:28,203 --> 00:44:31,706
I think I'm gonna trim the ears
and the beak off of that head.

425
00:44:33,041 --> 00:44:35,210
Make a nice pot of soup of that head.

426
00:44:35,376 --> 00:44:38,129
You could find a tastier head
than that, Bill.

427
00:44:38,296 --> 00:44:41,716
I ain't got the stomach for no Irish stew.

428
00:44:44,093 --> 00:44:49,224
The mighty McGloin
almost fishhooked by a sprat.

429
00:44:57,899 --> 00:45:02,070
On the seventh day, the Lord rested,
but before that he did...

430
00:45:02,237 --> 00:45:08,117
...he squatted over the side of England,
and what came out of him was Ireland.

431
00:45:08,284 --> 00:45:12,330
- No offense, son.
- Oh, none taken, sir. I grew up here.

432
00:45:12,497 --> 00:45:17,043
All I ever knew of Ireland was in the
talk of the others at the orphan asylum.

433
00:45:17,210 --> 00:45:22,173
In which part of that excrementitious
isle were your forebears spawned?

434
00:45:22,340 --> 00:45:27,971
I've been told Kerry, but I lost proof
of it in my language at the asylum.

435
00:45:28,137 --> 00:45:31,432
I was raised in a very similar
establishment myself.

436
00:45:31,599 --> 00:45:35,019
Now everything you see belongs to me,
to one degree or another.

437
00:45:35,186 --> 00:45:38,273
The beggars and newsboys
and quick thieves here in Paradise.

438
00:45:38,439 --> 00:45:41,985
The sailor dives and gin mills
and blind tigers on the waterfront.

439
00:45:42,151 --> 00:45:45,113
The anglers and amusers,
the she-hes and the Chinks.

440
00:45:45,280 --> 00:45:47,115
Everybody owes, everybody pays...

441
00:45:47,282 --> 00:45:53,162
...because that's how you stand up
against the rising of the tide.

442
00:45:53,329 --> 00:45:56,583
- Is that right, boys?
- Yeah, Bill. That's right.

443
00:45:56,749 --> 00:45:58,918
Enlist. Join up. Serve your country.

444
00:45:59,752 --> 00:46:02,547
Come on in out of the draft, boys.

445
00:46:02,714 --> 00:46:04,966
Volunteer and get your $50 bonus.

446
00:46:06,843 --> 00:46:11,139
We need 30,000 volunteers, and we're
prepared to pay $677 per volunteer.

447
00:46:11,306 --> 00:46:13,641
- Please read this. Thank you.
- Thanks.

448
00:46:13,808 --> 00:46:16,269
Would you like to take
one of these to fill out?

449
00:46:16,436 --> 00:46:19,981
Three square meals a day.
Three square meals a day.

450
00:46:20,148 --> 00:46:22,942
Young man, enlist
and serve your country.

451
00:46:23,109 --> 00:46:25,069
Three square meals a day, gentlemen.

452
00:46:25,236 --> 00:46:27,906
If you're interested,
read this and consider joining.

453
00:46:28,072 --> 00:46:30,909
Everywhere you went
people talked about the draft.

454
00:46:31,075 --> 00:46:34,287
Now, you could buy
your way out for $300...

455
00:46:34,454 --> 00:46:36,539
...but who had $300?

456
00:46:36,706 --> 00:46:39,500
For us it might as well
have been 3 million.

457
00:46:39,667 --> 00:46:43,671
The recruiters? They was too scared
of the gangs to come after us.

458
00:46:43,838 --> 00:46:48,676
Besides, we never dreamt the war
would ever touch New York.

459
00:46:48,843 --> 00:46:50,261
Good morning, sir.

460
00:46:52,222 --> 00:46:53,681
You!

461
00:46:54,140 --> 00:46:55,850
Don't you run into me.

462
00:46:57,185 --> 00:46:58,937
Fair enough.

463
00:47:03,733 --> 00:47:05,860
I said, don't run into me!

464
00:47:06,027 --> 00:47:08,196
Well, sorry.

465
00:47:13,993 --> 00:47:17,372
- Everything in place?
- It seems so.

466
00:47:17,539 --> 00:47:20,500
Well, then I leave you
in the grace and favor of the Lord.

467
00:47:20,667 --> 00:47:22,835
- Thank you.
- That's right.

468
00:47:30,385 --> 00:47:33,721
Oh, the dirty mab!

469
00:47:37,392 --> 00:47:38,977
Oh, no.

470
00:47:39,143 --> 00:47:41,604
- I'm sorry.
- Allow me.

471
00:47:43,898 --> 00:47:47,402
- Thank you kindly, sir.
- Oh, it's my pleasure.

472
00:47:48,152 --> 00:47:50,363
I hope you won't think
me rude if I speak.

473
00:47:50,530 --> 00:47:52,740
I wouldn't want to appear forward.

474
00:47:52,907 --> 00:47:57,745
Well, sir, that depends
on what you say.

475
00:48:05,670 --> 00:48:10,091
Would you call me reckless if I said
you were the prettiest girl in New York?

476
00:48:10,258 --> 00:48:12,969
Only New York?

477
00:48:14,804 --> 00:48:16,055
This is my stop.

478
00:48:18,182 --> 00:48:21,186
May I walk with you a little then?

479
00:48:21,352 --> 00:48:24,105
That, sir, would be too bold.

480
00:48:41,789 --> 00:48:42,874
Good morning.

481
00:48:51,299 --> 00:48:54,802
For every lay we had a different name.

482
00:48:54,969 --> 00:48:57,263
An "angler" put a hook
on the end of a stick...

483
00:48:57,430 --> 00:49:00,558
...to drop behind
store windows and doors.

484
00:49:00,725 --> 00:49:04,312
An "autumn diver"
picked your pocket in church.

485
00:49:04,479 --> 00:49:07,023
A "badger" gets a fellow
into bed with a girl...

486
00:49:07,190 --> 00:49:10,151
...then robs his pockets
while they're on the go.

487
00:49:10,652 --> 00:49:14,405
Jenny was a "bludget,"
a girl pickpocket...

488
00:49:14,572 --> 00:49:18,493
...and a "turtledove."

489
00:49:20,161 --> 00:49:23,373
A turtledove goes uptown
dressed like a housemaid...

490
00:49:23,540 --> 00:49:27,293
...picks out a fine house
and goes right through the back door.

491
00:49:27,710 --> 00:49:29,003
Robs you blind.

492
00:49:30,964 --> 00:49:34,259
It takes a lot of sand
to be a turtledove.

493
00:49:56,364 --> 00:50:00,326
I'll have my medal back. Oh, Jesus!

494
00:50:06,916 --> 00:50:08,918
Don't do that again.

495
00:50:09,085 --> 00:50:11,921
Listen! I said, don't do that...

496
00:50:12,589 --> 00:50:14,716
Go back to the Points
and leave me to my business...

497
00:50:14,883 --> 00:50:17,844
...or I will open your throat,
so help me God.

498
00:50:21,180 --> 00:50:23,141
All right.

499
00:50:24,726 --> 00:50:26,686
Go on then.

500
00:50:28,897 --> 00:50:30,815
I would.

501
00:50:32,358 --> 00:50:33,735
Go on then.

502
00:50:56,049 --> 00:50:58,718
Now give me back my medal.

503
00:50:59,093 --> 00:51:01,346
Make it quick!

504
00:51:08,478 --> 00:51:11,439
I don't know which one's yours.

505
00:51:18,029 --> 00:51:20,406
Suppose I help myself to everything?

506
00:51:23,201 --> 00:51:24,953
Suppose you do.

507
00:52:04,117 --> 00:52:06,911
May I walk with you a little then?

508
00:52:15,461 --> 00:52:18,172
What do you suppose a fellow
could earn here in a day?

509
00:52:18,506 --> 00:52:21,676
- Maybe we could pal in together.
- Oh, I think you're a bit...

510
00:52:21,843 --> 00:52:26,472
...rough for this sort of game.
Besides, I work alone.

511
00:52:26,639 --> 00:52:29,559
Alone? Well, what do you quarter
to the Butcher?

512
00:52:29,726 --> 00:52:32,020
- Me? Nothing.
- Nothing?

513
00:52:32,187 --> 00:52:34,689
The Butcher and me
have a special arrangement.

514
00:52:42,155 --> 00:52:45,992
- I don't want to see you again.
- Well, I don't blame you.

515
00:52:46,826 --> 00:52:49,537
Pennies in their pockets
and hope in their eyes...

516
00:52:49,704 --> 00:52:51,789
...they peer to the west...

517
00:52:51,956 --> 00:52:56,544
...searching the horizon
for a glimpse of land and salvation.

518
00:52:58,338 --> 00:52:59,923
A glimpse of America.

519
00:53:00,089 --> 00:53:03,426
Seeing all this poverty must be
most unsettling, Miss Schermerhorn.

520
00:53:03,593 --> 00:53:07,222
Some days, of course,
the uptown gangs come down to us.

521
00:53:07,805 --> 00:53:11,476
The Schermerhorns was one
of the oldest families in New York.

522
00:53:11,643 --> 00:53:13,228
They didn 't run the city...

523
00:53:13,394 --> 00:53:15,813
...but they was listened
to carefully by them that did.

524
00:53:15,980 --> 00:53:17,774
Commissioner Brunt
said you wished to view...

525
00:53:17,941 --> 00:53:20,318
...the Points in all
its splendor and squalor.

526
00:53:20,485 --> 00:53:22,737
"Spare nothing concerning
the conditions," said he.

527
00:53:22,904 --> 00:53:25,823
Yeah, nothing except
our safety, constable.

528
00:53:25,990 --> 00:53:29,577
I'm sure we can be in no danger
while we're in the constable's company.

529
00:53:29,744 --> 00:53:33,331
Quite so, madam. Witness.

530
00:53:40,463 --> 00:53:43,591
- Shall we continue on?
- What, you dare to leave it there?

531
00:53:43,758 --> 00:53:48,346
Safe as a bank, Mr. Greeley,
since all know it's mine.

532
00:53:48,513 --> 00:53:53,768
- Is that man drunk?
- Dead as Good friday, miss.

533
00:53:58,022 --> 00:54:00,400
- Good day to you, Mulraney.
- Boys.

534
00:54:00,567 --> 00:54:02,277
- Jack.
- Slum sociable?

535
00:54:02,443 --> 00:54:04,946
Fact-finding. Reforms-studying.

536
00:54:05,113 --> 00:54:07,574
- May I present...?
- The Schermerhorns of fifth Avenue...

537
00:54:07,740 --> 00:54:09,951
...scarcely require
an introduction from you.

538
00:54:10,118 --> 00:54:13,454
Schermerhorn, indeed. Their daughter.
This gentleman, of course...

539
00:54:13,621 --> 00:54:16,875
Horace Greeley, the famous publisher.
A pleasure and an honor.

540
00:54:17,292 --> 00:54:20,086
- Of the Tribune.
- I'm William Cutting.

541
00:54:21,462 --> 00:54:23,798
How do you do, Mr. Cutting?

542
00:54:25,300 --> 00:54:29,470
As of this moment, extremely well,
thank you. How do you do?

543
00:54:29,971 --> 00:54:32,015
Orange blossom. Delicious.

544
00:54:32,182 --> 00:54:34,475
- Mr. Cutting is...
- What a peach.

545
00:54:34,642 --> 00:54:36,644
She could have a fine career
on the stage.

546
00:54:36,811 --> 00:54:39,397
...one of Five Points' local...
- A pleasure to meet you.

547
00:54:39,564 --> 00:54:41,107
...leaders.
- Mr. Greeley.

548
00:54:41,274 --> 00:54:43,318
- Pleased to meet you.
- The Points welcomes you.

549
00:54:43,484 --> 00:54:46,446
You are welcome to these streets
and will pass in safety.

550
00:54:47,572 --> 00:54:52,327
Oh, I'll see to their safety.
Thank you, Bill. There we are.

551
00:54:52,493 --> 00:54:55,914
Good day, lads.
Criminal's braggadocio, you see.

552
00:54:56,080 --> 00:55:00,335
- He knows who I am.
- You're well-known in these parts.

553
00:55:00,668 --> 00:55:03,922
I must say that I find
that strangely flattering.

554
00:55:04,088 --> 00:55:06,799
- I never did like crushers.
- Well, draw it mild, son.

555
00:55:06,966 --> 00:55:11,512
Happy Jack don't fill his lungs
without I tell him he may do so.

556
00:55:24,067 --> 00:55:28,029
Do you think my watch
would be safe up on that lamppost, Bill?

557
00:55:29,364 --> 00:55:31,783
Why don't you hang it up there and see?

558
00:55:34,619 --> 00:55:35,787
Someday.

559
00:55:37,622 --> 00:55:39,123
Someday is right.

560
00:55:40,875 --> 00:55:42,961
This a new lad?

561
00:55:43,419 --> 00:55:48,925
Just another bastard son of Erin
I folded in the warmth of my embrace.

562
00:55:51,803 --> 00:55:54,430
- Hey!
- I just want to see your face, son.

563
00:55:54,597 --> 00:55:56,766
No harm intended.

564
00:56:19,873 --> 00:56:23,001
You get to know a lot butchering meat.

565
00:56:24,294 --> 00:56:26,838
We're made up of the same things:
Flesh and blood...

566
00:56:27,005 --> 00:56:30,300
...tissue, organs.

567
00:56:30,466 --> 00:56:33,177
I love to work with pigs.

568
00:56:33,344 --> 00:56:38,641
The nearest thing in nature to the flesh
of a man is the flesh of a pig.

569
00:56:38,808 --> 00:56:40,977
- Pig, huh?
- That's right.

570
00:56:41,144 --> 00:56:43,688
This is for you, mother.

571
00:56:46,191 --> 00:56:47,525
There you go.

572
00:56:47,692 --> 00:56:50,737
- God bless you, Mr. Cutting.
- God bless you.

573
00:56:50,904 --> 00:56:55,366
- She ain't really my mother.
- I knew that, Bill.

574
00:56:59,454 --> 00:57:01,789
This is the liver.

575
00:57:02,290 --> 00:57:04,083
The kidneys.

576
00:57:04,250 --> 00:57:06,085
The heart.

577
00:57:06,252 --> 00:57:09,631
This is a wound.
The stomach will bleed and bleed.

578
00:57:09,797 --> 00:57:13,134
This is a kill. This is a kill.

579
00:57:13,968 --> 00:57:17,388
The main artery. This is a kill.

580
00:57:18,765 --> 00:57:20,725
You try.

581
00:57:23,770 --> 00:57:25,230
Go ahead.

582
00:57:51,047 --> 00:57:53,550
The lung. Good.
Don't foul the blade on the rib.

583
00:57:53,716 --> 00:57:55,885
Very good. Main artery.

584
00:57:56,052 --> 00:57:58,847
Bleed him slow.
Let him think about it for a while.

585
00:57:59,013 --> 00:58:01,850
Slow death. Good.

586
00:58:03,268 --> 00:58:06,604
You'll each bill the city $5000
a month for supplies and services...

587
00:58:06,771 --> 00:58:08,565
...of which you'll receive 10%.

588
00:58:08,731 --> 00:58:12,569
Bill! Mr. Killoran will work out
the details. Thank you, gentlemen.

589
00:58:12,735 --> 00:58:15,780
That's how we do things
around Tammany, gentlemen.

590
00:58:15,947 --> 00:58:17,866
Who's this then?

591
00:58:18,867 --> 00:58:20,785
Thank you, boys.

592
00:58:20,952 --> 00:58:23,037
Good day, sir.

593
00:58:34,632 --> 00:58:37,176
You got something on your mind?

594
00:58:39,929 --> 00:58:42,015
Bill's taken quite a liking to you.

595
00:58:44,726 --> 00:58:48,521
Now, if you're up to something, bene.

596
00:58:48,688 --> 00:58:50,899
Only I don't want no part in it.

597
00:58:55,570 --> 00:58:59,073
I was in Hellgate for 16 years.

598
00:58:59,449 --> 00:59:01,826
I'm just trying to make my way is all.

599
00:59:01,993 --> 00:59:04,204
Just like you.

600
00:59:05,246 --> 00:59:07,415
Unless, of course,
you got a better notion?

601
00:59:08,917 --> 00:59:10,043
No.

602
00:59:11,002 --> 00:59:15,089
Bill, I can't get a day's work done
for all the good citizens...

603
00:59:15,256 --> 00:59:18,760
...coming in here to harass me
about crime in the Points.

604
00:59:18,927 --> 00:59:21,387
Some even go so far
as to accuse Tammany...

605
00:59:21,554 --> 00:59:25,308
...of connivance in this so-called
rampant criminality.

606
00:59:25,475 --> 00:59:28,603
What am I to do? I can't have this.
Something has to be done.

607
00:59:28,770 --> 00:59:31,147
- What do you have in mind?
- I don't know.

608
00:59:31,314 --> 00:59:33,608
I think maybe we should
hang someone.

609
00:59:34,859 --> 00:59:36,986
- Who?
- No one important, necessarily.

610
00:59:37,153 --> 00:59:41,741
Average men will do.
Back-alley amusers with no affiliations.

611
00:59:41,908 --> 00:59:45,620
- How many?
- Three or four.

612
00:59:45,954 --> 00:59:49,499
- Which?
- Four.

613
00:59:50,083 --> 00:59:53,670
You stand here convicted varyingly...

614
00:59:53,836 --> 00:59:56,965
...of lewdness, jack-rolling...

615
00:59:57,131 --> 01:00:00,885
...sneak-thievery,
chloral-hydrating...

616
01:00:01,052 --> 01:00:03,763
...sodomy, strangulation...

617
01:00:03,930 --> 01:00:08,852
...and enthusiastic corruption
of the public good.

618
01:00:10,311 --> 01:00:12,230
- We all go someday, huh?
- Chin up.

619
01:00:12,397 --> 01:00:14,607
Handsome bunch of gallows fruit.
There's my old friend.

620
01:00:14,774 --> 01:00:17,735
- How are you feeling, Arthur?
- I'm okay. I'm okay.

621
01:00:17,902 --> 01:00:20,947
Say hello to Amsterdam. Nearly
fishhooked McGloin the other day.

622
01:00:21,114 --> 01:00:22,282
- How do you do?
- Nearly.

623
01:00:22,448 --> 01:00:24,158
See you dressed for the occasion.

624
01:00:24,325 --> 01:00:26,494
You know me,
always like to look me best.

625
01:00:26,661 --> 01:00:28,162
That's the spirit.

626
01:00:29,664 --> 01:00:32,584
Nice locket.
I'll give you a dollar for it.

627
01:00:32,750 --> 01:00:34,419
Lt's me mother's.

628
01:00:34,586 --> 01:00:36,004
Dollar and a half?

629
01:00:36,963 --> 01:00:38,298
Done.

630
01:00:41,843 --> 01:00:43,344
We'll miss you, Arthur.

631
01:00:43,511 --> 01:00:45,680
- See you in the hot country, Seamus.
- Hot corn!

632
01:00:45,847 --> 01:00:49,017
Ears of early white corn!
Hot corn!

633
01:00:53,188 --> 01:00:55,190
Is my son present here?

634
01:00:56,941 --> 01:00:58,526
Where's my little fella?

635
01:01:00,695 --> 01:01:02,488
Look at your dad.

636
01:01:02,697 --> 01:01:04,699
Farewell, dear boy.

637
01:01:06,117 --> 01:01:10,997
I never struck a foul blow,
nor turned a card.

638
01:01:12,540 --> 01:01:16,044
May God greet me as a friend.

639
01:01:40,068 --> 01:01:43,571
And grace my fears relieved

640
01:01:43,738 --> 01:01:47,742
How precious
Did that grace appear

641
01:01:47,909 --> 01:01:50,745
That night,
the reformers held a dance.

642
01:01:50,912 --> 01:01:53,414
That was the Five Points, all right.

643
01:01:53,581 --> 01:01:56,876
Hangings of a morning,
dancings of an evening.

644
01:01:57,043 --> 01:01:58,962
- Sure meself, but...

645
01:01:59,128 --> 01:02:01,965
...it's not a matter of words.
I can tell by the way she smiles at me.

646
01:02:02,131 --> 01:02:04,092
- She smiles at a lot of people.
- Sir.

647
01:02:04,259 --> 01:02:05,552
You don't know her.

648
01:02:05,718 --> 01:02:09,264
A penny of gratitude
for a fighting Irishman, young sir.

649
01:02:12,475 --> 01:02:15,854
- You from Kerry?
- I am, sir. I am.

650
01:02:16,813 --> 01:02:19,440
- It ain't much.
- Thank you, sir. Thank you.

651
01:02:19,607 --> 01:02:22,193
Ladies and gentlemen,
we are delighted to see...

652
01:02:22,360 --> 01:02:25,822
...so many young faces
at our first annual Mission dance.

653
01:02:25,989 --> 01:02:27,866
This'll be a real ballum rancum, eh?

654
01:02:28,032 --> 01:02:31,077
The Mission welcomes Christians
of every stamp...

655
01:02:31,244 --> 01:02:34,831
...and we are particularly happy
to greet our Roman Catholic friends...

656
01:02:34,998 --> 01:02:36,958
...who join us here tonight.

657
01:02:37,208 --> 01:02:41,004
- Ladies! Come with me. Come with me.
- Shall we dance?

658
01:02:41,170 --> 01:02:44,007
We're going to start the dancing
in just a moment.

659
01:02:44,173 --> 01:02:45,216
No.

660
01:02:45,967 --> 01:02:49,804
- Good evening, Reverend.
- Good evening, Your Holiness.

661
01:02:51,890 --> 01:02:53,099
Good evening, Reverend.

662
01:02:53,433 --> 01:02:56,352
Looks as though
you should have shaved closer.

663
01:03:00,148 --> 01:03:03,484
Good evening, Reverend.
I do beg your pardon.

664
01:03:07,822 --> 01:03:09,657
Miss...?

665
01:03:13,494 --> 01:03:16,748
- Everdeane.
- Miss Everdeane.

666
01:03:28,885 --> 01:03:33,848
Now, gentlemen, over here,
if you don't mind. Thank you.

667
01:03:34,015 --> 01:03:36,851
Open it. Hold it up.

668
01:03:39,062 --> 01:03:41,022
Into the mirror.

669
01:04:33,741 --> 01:04:35,201
That one.

670
01:04:36,870 --> 01:04:40,206
Our queen has chosen.

671
01:04:58,766 --> 01:05:00,310
Sir...

672
01:05:00,476 --> 01:05:02,937
...your lady.
- Right.

673
01:05:03,104 --> 01:05:06,024
Regular services are held
at the church at 6 and 8.

674
01:05:06,191 --> 01:05:07,275
Go to hell.

675
01:05:16,701 --> 01:05:18,870
- What do you think you're doing?
- Dancing.

676
01:05:19,037 --> 01:05:22,290
- I said, why'd you pick me?
- That's none of your business.

677
01:05:22,457 --> 01:05:24,334
Would you mind telling me?

678
01:05:24,959 --> 01:05:26,461
I'm sorry.

679
01:05:27,420 --> 01:05:29,505
Goddamn it!

680
01:05:30,089 --> 01:05:33,801
I'm sorry.
I'm not much of a dancer.

681
01:05:33,968 --> 01:05:35,803
Just try to keep calm.

682
01:05:35,970 --> 01:05:40,225
- I never been to a stepping-ken before.
- Lt's best not to look down.

683
01:05:42,352 --> 01:05:45,021
You can hold me tighter if you like.

684
01:05:52,529 --> 01:05:54,489
- All right?
- Aye.

685
01:05:56,324 --> 01:05:59,160
So why didn't you dance with Johnny?

686
01:06:00,578 --> 01:06:02,121
Because...

687
01:06:04,707 --> 01:06:06,876
...I didn't want him.

688
01:07:05,143 --> 01:07:07,437
I'm not wanting this.

689
01:07:14,652 --> 01:07:15,695
- Wait.
- What?

690
01:07:15,862 --> 01:07:17,405
Wait.

691
01:07:18,281 --> 01:07:19,490
How does this open?

692
01:07:19,657 --> 01:07:23,661
It takes too long to lace back up.
We'll be here all night.

693
01:07:35,089 --> 01:07:38,009
Sure, all right, I'll take it off.

694
01:07:59,489 --> 01:08:02,325
There was a baby.
They cut it out.

695
01:08:06,704 --> 01:08:08,540
I'm sorry.

696
01:08:09,624 --> 01:08:11,668
Oh, it's fine.

697
01:08:15,463 --> 01:08:17,924
Have you got any scars?

698
01:08:19,425 --> 01:08:21,219
One or two.

699
01:09:19,402 --> 01:09:21,362
What's that, then?

700
01:09:21,529 --> 01:09:22,906
That?

701
01:09:23,114 --> 01:09:25,533
Lt's a gift from Mr. Cutting.

702
01:09:29,871 --> 01:09:31,623
A gift?

703
01:09:32,290 --> 01:09:34,167
A gift.

704
01:09:35,335 --> 01:09:36,878
Was it your birthday?

705
01:09:38,213 --> 01:09:39,881
No.

706
01:09:44,052 --> 01:09:46,429
What'd you give him, then?

707
01:09:48,556 --> 01:09:51,392
The answer to that
has nothing to do with you.

708
01:09:58,149 --> 01:10:00,693
- Don't tell me you're angry with me.
- No.

709
01:10:01,236 --> 01:10:03,404
I'm through with you.

710
01:10:07,408 --> 01:10:10,119
Well, you're quicker
than most fellows.

711
01:10:10,286 --> 01:10:12,580
Generally, they wait till afterwards.

712
01:10:12,747 --> 01:10:15,750
I ain't interested
in the Butcher's leavings.

713
01:10:35,478 --> 01:10:38,022
Hey! Bill says
keep clear of the pikers.

714
01:10:38,189 --> 01:10:41,109
- Fifteen to one, Bug-Eye Moran.
- Amsterdam!

715
01:10:41,943 --> 01:10:44,279
Everyone was working
for the Butcher.

716
01:10:44,445 --> 01:10:46,281
We ran his errands,
made his money...

717
01:10:46,447 --> 01:10:48,449
...took a piece and said,
"Thank you, sir."

718
01:10:48,616 --> 01:10:51,202
- Gentlemen, it's a raid!
- Even Tweed.

719
01:10:51,369 --> 01:10:53,788
- The fight's over.
- Even me.

720
01:10:53,955 --> 01:10:56,624
- All right, you stupid...
- My father's son.

721
01:10:57,041 --> 01:10:59,252
- What the hell is this?
- Sorry, Bill.

722
01:10:59,419 --> 01:11:03,256
The city ordinance against boxiana's
a blight, I grant you, but some...

723
01:11:03,423 --> 01:11:06,426
I'm losing revenue while you speechify.
Collect those bets!

724
01:11:06,593 --> 01:11:08,636
How do I collect
if no one won the fight?

725
01:11:08,803 --> 01:11:11,639
This counts as a "no decision."
You got that covered, right?

726
01:11:11,806 --> 01:11:13,725
Didn't anybody pay off the police?

727
01:11:13,892 --> 01:11:16,561
We paid the Municipal Police.
This is Metropolitan.

728
01:11:16,728 --> 01:11:18,771
- You'll repay me every cent.
- Mr. Tweed...

729
01:11:18,938 --> 01:11:21,149
- Who asked for your opinion?
- Let him speak!

730
01:11:21,316 --> 01:11:22,650
He don't answer to you.

731
01:11:22,942 --> 01:11:25,486
The law says there's no boxing
in the city, right?

732
01:11:25,653 --> 01:11:28,948
- That's right. In the city.
- Well, where does the city end?

733
01:11:36,080 --> 01:11:40,168
The winner, in the 75th round...

734
01:11:40,335 --> 01:11:45,256
Allow me to acknowledge the man whose
vision and enterprise made possible...

735
01:11:45,423 --> 01:11:48,259
...the staging of this noble combat...

736
01:11:48,426 --> 01:11:52,430
...in these awe-inspiring
and entirely legal surroundings:

737
01:11:52,597 --> 01:11:55,600
Mr. William Cutting!

738
01:11:58,102 --> 01:12:02,482
And... And his young associate!

739
01:12:02,857 --> 01:12:04,567
That's you, boy.

740
01:12:06,528 --> 01:12:08,363
Come on, get in there.
Come on, son.

741
01:12:11,074 --> 01:12:13,618
You done very good.
How do you like them apples?

742
01:12:13,785 --> 01:12:15,620
We done good.
We done all right.

743
01:12:16,162 --> 01:12:18,831
- While I have your attention...
- It was a good show.

744
01:12:18,998 --> 01:12:21,376
...may I remind you of the wonders
that await you...

745
01:12:21,543 --> 01:12:25,505
...at my muzeum located on Broadway!

746
01:12:25,672 --> 01:12:29,050
P.T. Barnum's Gallery of Wonders!

747
01:12:31,344 --> 01:12:35,390
- That's my money!
- Get your hands out of me pockets!

748
01:12:37,976 --> 01:12:42,063
Lt's a funny feeling being took
under the wing of a dragon.

749
01:12:42,397 --> 01:12:44,816
Lt's warmer than you think.

750
01:12:45,692 --> 01:12:48,736
That's the building of our country
right there, Mr. Cutting.

751
01:12:48,903 --> 01:12:50,780
Americans aborning.

752
01:12:50,947 --> 01:12:54,617
I don't see no Americans.
I see trespassers.

753
01:12:54,784 --> 01:12:58,413
Irish harps will do a job for a nickel
what a nigger does for a dime...

754
01:12:58,580 --> 01:13:00,540
...and a white man used to get
a quarter for.

755
01:13:00,707 --> 01:13:03,084
What have they done?
Name one thing they've contributed.

756
01:13:03,251 --> 01:13:05,670
- Votes.
- Votes, you say?

757
01:13:05,837 --> 01:13:08,923
They vote how the archbishop
tells them. And who tells him?

758
01:13:09,090 --> 01:13:13,344
Their king in the pointy hat
what sits on his throne in Rome.

759
01:13:13,511 --> 01:13:16,556
Bill's got mixed feelings
as regards the Irish.

760
01:13:17,557 --> 01:13:21,769
Deliver these good and fervent folk
to the polls on a regular basis...

761
01:13:21,936 --> 01:13:26,441
...and there'll be a handsome price
for each vote goes Tammany's way.

762
01:13:28,902 --> 01:13:32,864
My father gave his life
making this country what it is.

763
01:13:33,031 --> 01:13:38,286
Murdered by the British with all his men
on the 25th of July, anno Domini 1814.

764
01:13:38,453 --> 01:13:41,247
You think I'm gonna help you
befoul his legacy...

765
01:13:41,414 --> 01:13:45,043
...by giving this country over to them
what's had no hand in fighting for it?

766
01:13:45,210 --> 01:13:49,214
Why? Because they come off a boat,
crawling with lice and begging for soup?

767
01:13:50,048 --> 01:13:54,135
You're a great one for the fighting,
I know, but you can't fight forever.

768
01:13:54,302 --> 01:13:57,555
- I can go down doing it.
- And you will.

769
01:14:01,059 --> 01:14:03,061
What did you say?

770
01:14:03,228 --> 01:14:06,397
I said, you're turning your back
on the future.

771
01:14:08,608 --> 01:14:10,818
Not our future.

772
01:14:16,199 --> 01:14:20,161
That document makes you a citizen. This
makes you a private in the Union Army.

773
01:14:20,328 --> 01:14:22,163
Now go fight for your country.

774
01:14:22,330 --> 01:14:24,040
Next.

775
01:14:24,415 --> 01:14:27,335
Sign here, son.
Or make your mark.

776
01:14:27,502 --> 01:14:29,837
You're in the Army now.
Three square meals a day.

777
01:14:30,004 --> 01:14:33,174
Uniforms first, then weapons.

778
01:14:33,925 --> 01:14:36,803
- I haven't eaten in two days.
- Keep moving, men.

779
01:14:45,520 --> 01:14:48,147
There's your musket.
Keep it dry on the boat.

780
01:14:48,314 --> 01:14:50,733
- Same for this cartridge case.
- Where are we going?

781
01:14:50,900 --> 01:14:52,652
- I heard Tennessee.
- Where's that?

782
01:15:06,207 --> 01:15:09,210
Do they feed us now, do you think?

783
01:15:18,553 --> 01:15:23,141
My children! My children!

784
01:15:23,349 --> 01:15:27,645
We must heal the divisions
between us.

785
01:15:27,812 --> 01:15:28,897
This war must cease.

786
01:15:31,941 --> 01:15:35,069
North and South must stand united!

787
01:15:39,616 --> 01:15:42,911
You're a liar! Rubbish!

788
01:15:43,995 --> 01:15:49,250
- What happens at the finish, then?
- Then we have ourselves a rowdy-dow.

789
01:15:49,667 --> 01:15:53,171
- Ain't you been to the theater before?
- No.

790
01:15:53,338 --> 01:15:57,425
Mr. Legree, lay down your whip.

791
01:15:57,592 --> 01:16:01,429
Miss Eliza,
join hands with Mr. Shelby.

792
01:16:01,596 --> 01:16:08,019
And, Topsy, dear little Topsy,
cradle Uncle Tom's head.

793
01:16:08,603 --> 01:16:10,605
Leave the nigger dead!

794
01:16:20,615 --> 01:16:21,741
Get me down!

795
01:16:21,908 --> 01:16:24,953
Down with the Union!

796
01:16:25,119 --> 01:16:26,788
Down with the Union!

797
01:16:26,955 --> 01:16:28,623
Down with the Union!

798
01:16:28,790 --> 01:16:30,959
- Down with the Union!
- Down with the Union!

799
01:16:49,227 --> 01:16:51,646
- For the blood of the Irish!
- Bill, get down!

800
01:17:00,822 --> 01:17:03,658
Somebody's popped the Butcher!

801
01:17:14,377 --> 01:17:17,672
Stifle that rat bastard down!

802
01:17:24,012 --> 01:17:26,681
God, forgive me.

803
01:17:44,365 --> 01:17:45,992
Whose man are you?

804
01:17:46,159 --> 01:17:48,703
Speak smart and speak up.

805
01:17:49,704 --> 01:17:51,164
What's he saying, boy?

806
01:17:53,416 --> 01:17:55,460
I think he's making his peace
with God.

807
01:17:55,627 --> 01:17:57,837
The hell with that.
He makes peace with me.

808
01:17:58,004 --> 01:18:01,633
I'm hearing confession tonight,
you mother-whoring Irish nigger!

809
01:18:01,799 --> 01:18:04,469
Whose man are you?
We speak English in this country.

810
01:18:04,636 --> 01:18:06,387
Whose man are you?
See this knife?

811
01:18:06,554 --> 01:18:09,641
I'm gonna teach you to speak English
with this fucking knife!

812
01:18:09,807 --> 01:18:11,351
Whose man are you?

813
01:18:11,518 --> 01:18:13,770
Whose man are you?

814
01:18:28,993 --> 01:18:32,247
Well, that didn't tell us very much.

815
01:18:34,791 --> 01:18:36,960
Fine waistcoat.

816
01:18:37,460 --> 01:18:41,089
Shame about it.
I don't think it can be mended.

817
01:18:41,506 --> 01:18:44,843
Will I keep it as a souvenir?

818
01:19:18,209 --> 01:19:21,045
Where's Legree? Where's Mopsy?

819
01:19:21,212 --> 01:19:25,967
Tragedians, let's continue.
Intermission is over!

820
01:19:43,276 --> 01:19:46,112
Now that was bloody Shakespearean.

821
01:19:46,279 --> 01:19:49,824
- What?
- Do you know who Shakespeare was?

822
01:19:50,325 --> 01:19:52,911
He was the fellow who wrote
the King James Bible.

823
01:19:53,077 --> 01:19:55,330
I don't know what you're talking about.

824
01:19:55,496 --> 01:19:58,458
That's because you're a thick,
ignorant, barbarous Irish whelp.

825
01:19:58,625 --> 01:20:00,418
Just like your father.

826
01:20:04,714 --> 01:20:06,007
That's it.

827
01:20:06,174 --> 01:20:10,595
Tear my head off and destruct the world,
just like the rest of the stupid Irish.

828
01:20:10,762 --> 01:20:16,184
- That's why I never ran with your dad.
- Get off me, you crazy bastard!

829
01:20:19,020 --> 01:20:22,690
It means, if you're not strong,
you'd better be smart.

830
01:20:22,857 --> 01:20:25,652
I don't know if you're being
too clever or too dumb.

831
01:20:25,818 --> 01:20:27,946
Whichever it is,
just remember this much.

832
01:20:28,112 --> 01:20:33,368
For all his faults, your father
was a man who loved his people.

833
01:20:39,541 --> 01:20:42,293
Amsterdam! Amsterdam!

834
01:20:42,460 --> 01:20:45,129
New York is calling you.

835
01:20:56,641 --> 01:21:00,812
Look at that.
What in Christ's name is that?

836
01:21:01,479 --> 01:21:06,943
Rhythms of the Dark Continent thrown
into the kettle with an Irish shindig.

837
01:21:07,110 --> 01:21:12,031
Stir it around a few times, pull it out,
it's a fine American mess.

838
01:21:12,407 --> 01:21:14,909
A jig doing a jig.

839
01:21:15,952 --> 01:21:17,912
A little keepsake.

840
01:21:21,207 --> 01:21:23,585
- Good on you, sir.
- Come upstairs with me, Bill.

841
01:21:25,545 --> 01:21:28,131
Have I ever had you before?

842
01:21:29,591 --> 01:21:32,093
So you don't call me
by my Christian name.

843
01:21:33,553 --> 01:21:35,763
Bill! Thank God you're all right.

844
01:21:35,930 --> 01:21:38,141
I heard the news
and came as quick as I could.

845
01:21:38,308 --> 01:21:40,476
You're as timely as the Angel of Death.

846
01:21:40,643 --> 01:21:45,273
Bill, you're not suggesting that I would
stoop to what you're suggesting?

847
01:21:45,440 --> 01:21:49,110
Stop slabbering. If I thought it was you,
you'd be in a wooden coat.

848
01:21:49,277 --> 01:21:52,614
The hole in my shoulder hurts,
so have a drink and shut up.

849
01:21:52,780 --> 01:21:55,116
Or shut up and get out.

850
01:21:55,491 --> 01:21:57,035
I believe I'll have a drink.

851
01:21:57,202 --> 01:21:59,287
Careful, Tweedy,
the mort's frenchified.

852
01:22:00,496 --> 01:22:03,958
No, no, I'm clean.
Come on, let's go to the bar.

853
01:22:16,804 --> 01:22:18,723
- Jenny.
- Bill.

854
01:22:42,997 --> 01:22:45,083
Lt's gotta be tight.

855
01:22:45,250 --> 01:22:47,335
She loves to make me cry.

856
01:22:48,211 --> 01:22:49,671
You can take it.

857
01:22:58,805 --> 01:23:00,849
There's my boy.

858
01:23:06,855 --> 01:23:08,439
Lt's all right.

859
01:23:14,112 --> 01:23:16,114
To the Butcher.

860
01:23:20,577 --> 01:23:22,829
We're all much obliged.

861
01:23:24,414 --> 01:23:25,707
Forever.

862
01:23:25,874 --> 01:23:28,710
- Bill the Butcher.
- To the Butcher.

863
01:23:37,927 --> 01:23:39,971
Why don't you get out of here, Johnny?

864
01:23:43,224 --> 01:23:44,392
Go on. Go.

865
01:24:01,951 --> 01:24:05,038
Is there anyone in the Five Points
you haven't fucked?

866
01:24:05,205 --> 01:24:06,664
You!

867
01:24:17,133 --> 01:24:19,219
Calm down!

868
01:24:30,438 --> 01:24:32,565
Try it and I'll bite you!

869
01:24:32,732 --> 01:24:36,277
If you were gonna bite me,
I don't think you'd warn me, huh?

870
01:24:36,444 --> 01:24:38,238
Find out.

871
01:25:56,399 --> 01:25:58,026
I can't sleep.

872
01:26:04,240 --> 01:26:08,369
I hope you don't mind us
laying out tonight here, sir.

873
01:26:09,204 --> 01:26:12,123
Whatever takes your fancy,
my young friend.

874
01:26:20,548 --> 01:26:25,053
Is it your shoulder
that's keeping you up?

875
01:26:25,220 --> 01:26:27,972
No, I don't never sleep too much.

876
01:26:28,348 --> 01:26:32,727
I have to sleep with one eye open.
I only got one eye, right?

877
01:26:37,398 --> 01:26:40,235
How old are you, Amsterdam?

878
01:26:44,989 --> 01:26:47,325
I'm not too sure.

879
01:26:47,492 --> 01:26:49,244
I never did figure it.

880
01:26:50,578 --> 01:26:52,789
I'm 47.

881
01:26:54,707 --> 01:26:57,085
Forty-seven years old.

882
01:27:00,839 --> 01:27:04,509
You know how I stayed alive this long?
All these years?

883
01:27:07,095 --> 01:27:09,097
Fear.

884
01:27:10,765 --> 01:27:13,768
The spectacle of fearsome acts.

885
01:27:14,769 --> 01:27:17,939
Somebody steals from me,
I cut off his hands.

886
01:27:18,106 --> 01:27:21,234
He offends me,
I cut out his tongue.

887
01:27:21,401 --> 01:27:26,906
He rises against me,
I cut off his head, stick it on a pike...

888
01:27:27,073 --> 01:27:31,119
...and raise it high up
so all in the streets can see.

889
01:27:32,203 --> 01:27:35,623
That's what preserves
the order of things.

890
01:27:39,169 --> 01:27:40,879
Fear.

891
01:27:43,506 --> 01:27:45,300
That one tonight...

892
01:27:45,466 --> 01:27:47,594
...who was he?

893
01:27:49,179 --> 01:27:51,181
A nobody.

894
01:27:52,140 --> 01:27:54,142
A coward.

895
01:27:55,768 --> 01:27:59,772
What an ignominious end
that would have been.

896
01:28:02,775 --> 01:28:08,114
I killed the last honorable man
15 years ago. Since then, it's...

897
01:28:10,325 --> 01:28:13,328
You've seen his portrait downstairs?

898
01:28:16,664 --> 01:28:20,001
Is your mouth glued up with
cunny juice? I asked you a question!

899
01:28:20,168 --> 01:28:22,462
I said, I seen it, sir.

900
01:28:27,717 --> 01:28:30,845
Oh, you got a murderous rage
in you, and I like it.

901
01:28:31,012 --> 01:28:35,183
Lt's life boiling up inside of you.
Lt's good.

902
01:28:38,728 --> 01:28:43,191
The Priest and me,
we lived by the same principles.

903
01:28:45,527 --> 01:28:48,655
It was only faith divided us.

904
01:28:51,032 --> 01:28:52,825
He give me this, you know.

905
01:28:58,540 --> 01:29:01,876
That was the finest beating
I ever took.

906
01:29:02,502 --> 01:29:04,045
My face was pulp...

907
01:29:04,212 --> 01:29:08,007
...my guts was pierced
and my ribs was all mashed up.

908
01:29:12,554 --> 01:29:15,348
And when he came to finish me...

909
01:29:19,310 --> 01:29:22,105
...I couldn't look him in the eye.

910
01:29:28,027 --> 01:29:32,907
He spared me because
he wanted me to live in shame.

911
01:29:33,074 --> 01:29:35,577
This was a great man.

912
01:29:38,246 --> 01:29:40,290
A great man.

913
01:29:43,209 --> 01:29:46,421
So I cut out the eye that looked away.

914
01:29:49,257 --> 01:29:51,759
Sent it to him, wrapped in blue paper.

915
01:29:52,677 --> 01:29:56,431
I would have cut them both out
if I could have fought him blind.

916
01:29:58,433 --> 01:30:01,519
Then I rose back up again
with a full heart...

917
01:30:01,686 --> 01:30:04,522
...and buried him in his own blood.

918
01:30:13,531 --> 01:30:15,533
Well done.

919
01:30:18,411 --> 01:30:22,040
He was the only man I ever killed
worth remembering.

920
01:30:33,635 --> 01:30:36,179
I never had a son.

921
01:30:44,354 --> 01:30:47,273
Civilization is crumbling.

922
01:31:05,500 --> 01:31:07,836
God bless you.

923
01:31:38,658 --> 01:31:41,411
If you got anything to say...

924
01:31:41,995 --> 01:31:44,747
...now is the time to say it.

925
01:31:44,914 --> 01:31:47,125
Who is he to you, Jenny?

926
01:31:53,339 --> 01:31:55,925
I was 12 years old.

927
01:31:56,092 --> 01:31:59,846
My mother was dead.
I was living in a doorway.

928
01:32:02,182 --> 01:32:06,686
He took me in, took care of me...

929
01:32:07,520 --> 01:32:09,606
...in his own way.

930
01:32:14,652 --> 01:32:17,322
After they cut out the baby...

931
01:32:19,866 --> 01:32:23,536
Well, he doesn't fancy girls
that scarred up.

932
01:32:30,376 --> 01:32:34,881
You should know in your own mind...

933
01:32:36,090 --> 01:32:40,178
...that he never laid a hand on me
until I asked him to.

934
01:33:02,617 --> 01:33:04,911
Who are you?

935
01:33:05,787 --> 01:33:08,289
Who are you?

936
01:34:24,991 --> 01:34:28,578
Gentlemen. Fine gentlemen,
you are most welcome...

937
01:34:28,745 --> 01:34:32,499
...to this palace of wonder
and enchantment...

938
01:34:32,665 --> 01:34:37,670
...where visions to rival the finest
imaginings of Scheherezade...

939
01:34:37,837 --> 01:34:40,840
...and her tales from
the perfumed nights of Arabia...

940
01:34:41,007 --> 01:34:45,011
...will greet your famished eyes.

941
01:35:03,655 --> 01:35:07,158
What am I bid for this
flaxen-haired Teutonic beauty?

942
01:35:07,659 --> 01:35:12,997
Feast your eyes on the magnificent
plumage of these exotic creatures.

943
01:35:13,164 --> 01:35:16,125
Gentlemen, you've seen
our caged birds.

944
01:35:16,292 --> 01:35:20,505
Well, how'd you like
to make them sing?

945
01:35:20,672 --> 01:35:26,845
Let me see the color of your money,
and they could be singing for you.

946
01:35:32,851 --> 01:35:36,354
May God put the steel of the Holy Spirit
in my spine...

947
01:35:37,647 --> 01:35:41,192
...and the love of the Blessed Virgin
in my heart.

948
01:35:44,153 --> 01:35:46,155
Amen.

949
01:35:49,993 --> 01:35:51,995
Easy now.

950
01:35:52,161 --> 01:35:53,997
What did you say?

951
01:35:54,164 --> 01:35:56,666
He's not who he says he is.

952
01:35:57,542 --> 01:35:59,335
He's not who he says he is?

953
01:35:59,502 --> 01:36:03,548
What am I bid? What am I bid?
What am I bid?

954
01:36:03,923 --> 01:36:07,343
Count your blessings
he considers you a friend.

955
01:36:08,511 --> 01:36:13,683
The only thing he considers, every day
and every night, is how best to kill you.

956
01:36:15,018 --> 01:36:18,855
You got a loose tongue in your head.
You want me to take it out for you?

957
01:36:19,022 --> 01:36:22,275
I'll bury it right up your fundament
where it belongs.

958
01:36:22,442 --> 01:36:24,694
I can read right through you.
You're empty...

959
01:36:24,861 --> 01:36:27,280
His name is Vallon!

960
01:36:28,865 --> 01:36:32,869
Don't do it, Bill. His name's Vallon.

961
01:37:04,400 --> 01:37:07,111
They're curious people,
these Celestials.

962
01:37:07,278 --> 01:37:08,571
Don't trust this guy.

963
01:37:08,738 --> 01:37:10,740
- Know why he wears short sleeves?
- Why?

964
01:37:10,907 --> 01:37:13,243
So everyone can see
he's got nothing stashed.

965
01:37:13,493 --> 01:37:16,621
Jesus, let's hope that never
becomes the fashion.

966
01:37:22,961 --> 01:37:24,796
Johnny.

967
01:38:02,083 --> 01:38:03,877
Get up.

968
01:38:18,600 --> 01:38:23,855
Ladies and gentlemen,
if I may have your kind attention.

969
01:38:24,022 --> 01:38:26,524
As some of you have surely noticed...

970
01:38:26,691 --> 01:38:30,695
...our friend and benefactor
Mr. William Cutting...

971
01:38:30,862 --> 01:38:37,243
...is tonight wearing a waistcoat
of certain distinction.

972
01:38:38,369 --> 01:38:39,996
Might we...

973
01:38:41,664 --> 01:38:45,210
Might we tonight appeal
to him to favor us...

974
01:38:45,376 --> 01:38:49,631
...with another exciting exhibition
of skill...

975
01:38:49,797 --> 01:38:55,136
...courage, daring and drama!

976
01:39:07,398 --> 01:39:12,070
- The Sword of Damascus!
- The Tomahawk!

977
01:39:13,738 --> 01:39:15,406
Bill's going up.

978
01:39:22,413 --> 01:39:24,332
Show us your knives.

979
01:39:24,499 --> 01:39:27,043
Strut your stuff, Billy boy.

980
01:39:27,210 --> 01:39:29,587
Sword of Damascus!

981
01:39:43,434 --> 01:39:46,604
- The Spider's Kiss!
- The River of Blood!

982
01:39:46,771 --> 01:39:50,400
- The Morning Glory!
- The Wheel of Death!

983
01:39:50,567 --> 01:39:52,777
- William Tell!
- The Butterfly!

984
01:39:53,111 --> 01:39:54,904
- The Tomahawk!
- The Spider's Kiss!

985
01:39:55,280 --> 01:39:57,115
The Butcher's Apprentice!

986
01:39:57,282 --> 01:39:58,658
The Butcher's Apprentice.

987
01:39:58,825 --> 01:40:02,120
- Command performance.
- Command performance.

988
01:40:02,370 --> 01:40:04,622
- Command performance.
- Where have you been?

989
01:40:04,789 --> 01:40:07,292
A command performance indeed!

990
01:40:07,458 --> 01:40:09,127
- Get out of here.
- Don't do it.

991
01:40:09,294 --> 01:40:13,923
And for this I must beg the indulgence
of my former assistant...

992
01:40:14,090 --> 01:40:16,551
...in matters of impalement.

993
01:40:16,718 --> 01:40:20,138
The Butcher's original apprentice.

994
01:40:20,972 --> 01:40:26,102
What do you say, Jen?
One more time for the sweet souvenir?

995
01:40:26,269 --> 01:40:28,146
Come on.

996
01:40:37,488 --> 01:40:39,616
Don't you do it.

997
01:40:40,283 --> 01:40:42,493
Good girl, Jenny.

998
01:40:42,660 --> 01:40:46,498
- Don't be afraid.
- What a woman.

999
01:40:49,125 --> 01:40:50,960
Good evening, Bill.

1000
01:40:51,127 --> 01:40:53,171
She'll be all right.

1001
01:40:55,965 --> 01:40:59,135
- Remember this?
- Of course.

1002
01:41:07,852 --> 01:41:13,024
You may feel more comfortable
without that garment, Miss Everdeane.

1003
01:41:14,192 --> 01:41:17,362
You'll have to filch me a new one, Bill.

1004
01:41:30,667 --> 01:41:33,169
Anything in your pockets tonight?

1005
01:41:33,336 --> 01:41:35,672
Oh, I ain't started working yet.

1006
01:41:38,842 --> 01:41:41,719
What about that locket
that I gave you?

1007
01:41:57,026 --> 01:42:00,405
Apologies, my dear. Pick it up.

1008
01:42:05,243 --> 01:42:07,203
Whoopsie-daisy!

1009
01:42:09,205 --> 01:42:11,708
Now it's good and broke.

1010
01:42:12,709 --> 01:42:15,211
I can't seem to get anything
right tonight.

1011
01:42:22,886 --> 01:42:26,723
You got the sand to give them
a grand finale?

1012
01:42:34,898 --> 01:42:38,151
Maybe when you're aiming
a little straighter.

1013
01:42:45,825 --> 01:42:50,413
Still got some blood in you, Jen?
Enough of this heathen music!

1014
01:42:50,580 --> 01:42:53,750
Get rid of these goddamn monkeys.
Strike up, boys!

1015
01:42:53,917 --> 01:42:59,339
Ready? This is a night for Americans!

1016
01:43:06,346 --> 01:43:08,848
- Jenny!
- Get your hands off me.

1017
01:43:09,015 --> 01:43:12,101
- Go away.
- Get your hands off me!

1018
01:43:15,146 --> 01:43:16,439
Move.

1019
01:43:16,606 --> 01:43:18,691
Amsterdam!

1020
01:44:03,862 --> 01:44:06,072
Hats off!

1021
01:44:12,579 --> 01:44:17,292
We hold in our hearts the memory
of our fallen brothers...

1022
01:44:17,542 --> 01:44:21,045
...whose blood stains the very streets
we walk today.

1023
01:44:22,630 --> 01:44:28,386
Also on this night, we pay tribute
to the leader of our enemies...

1024
01:44:30,138 --> 01:44:35,059
...an honorable man
who crossed over bravely...

1025
01:44:35,226 --> 01:44:37,896
...fighting for what he believed in.

1026
01:44:44,068 --> 01:44:48,740
To defeat my enemy,
I extinguish his life...

1027
01:44:48,907 --> 01:44:52,911
...and consume him
as I consume these flames.

1028
01:44:54,329 --> 01:44:56,581
In honor of Priest Vallon.

1029
01:45:05,089 --> 01:45:06,633
That's a wound.

1030
01:45:07,008 --> 01:45:09,344
God!

1031
01:45:15,892 --> 01:45:21,022
I want you all to meet
the son of Priest Vallon.

1032
01:45:21,189 --> 01:45:23,858
I took him under my wing...

1033
01:45:24,025 --> 01:45:25,777
...and see how I'm repaid?

1034
01:45:25,944 --> 01:45:28,112
- You bastard!
- Saves my life one day...

1035
01:45:28,279 --> 01:45:30,865
...so he can kill me the next
like a sneak-thief...

1036
01:45:31,032 --> 01:45:34,035
...instead of fighting like a man.

1037
01:45:34,452 --> 01:45:38,748
A base defiler,
unworthy of a noble name.

1038
01:45:41,125 --> 01:45:43,586
God! Jesus!

1039
01:45:51,970 --> 01:45:55,056
All right, that'll do, McGloin.
Splay him out.

1040
01:45:56,182 --> 01:46:00,979
This show ain't for blackies.
You! You get to watch.

1041
01:46:02,480 --> 01:46:05,149
Don't you hear, nigger?

1042
01:46:06,568 --> 01:46:10,321
This is fresh meat.
You know what I mean?

1043
01:46:10,488 --> 01:46:13,825
We need to tenderize this meat
a little bit.

1044
01:46:13,992 --> 01:46:17,829
All right, let's kiss good night
to that pretty young face of yours.

1045
01:46:22,458 --> 01:46:23,668
No!

1046
01:46:28,214 --> 01:46:30,175
No!

1047
01:46:31,301 --> 01:46:35,180
What'll it be, then? Rib or chop?
Loin or shank?

1048
01:46:35,346 --> 01:46:37,348
- The liver!
- The spleen!

1049
01:46:37,515 --> 01:46:39,225
- The tongue!
- Kidneys!

1050
01:46:45,523 --> 01:46:47,692
- The lungs!
- The liver!

1051
01:46:47,859 --> 01:46:49,694
- The tongue!
- The kidneys!

1052
01:46:49,861 --> 01:46:52,197
- The stomach!
- The heart! The heart!

1053
01:46:52,363 --> 01:46:54,866
The heart? This boy has no heart.

1054
01:46:55,033 --> 01:46:57,827
- Then kill him!
- Kill him!

1055
01:47:03,041 --> 01:47:07,712
He ain't earned a death.
He ain't earned a death at my hands.

1056
01:47:08,004 --> 01:47:12,509
No. He'll walk amongst you
marked with shame...

1057
01:47:12,675 --> 01:47:18,056
...a freak worthy of Barnum's
Muzeum of Wonders.

1058
01:47:18,223 --> 01:47:21,684
Go�s only man spared
by the Butcher.

1059
01:47:21,851 --> 01:47:23,269
No, Bill!

1060
01:47:24,437 --> 01:47:27,440
No! No!

1061
01:47:27,774 --> 01:47:31,277
No! No! No!

1062
01:47:31,444 --> 01:47:34,906
No! No! No!

1063
01:48:01,307 --> 01:48:03,476
Here. Here!

1064
01:48:03,643 --> 01:48:06,104
This way. Come on. Come on!

1065
01:48:52,150 --> 01:48:54,444
I wanna show you something.

1066
01:48:59,324 --> 01:49:02,494
My mother's bones share this grave.

1067
01:49:04,496 --> 01:49:09,167
I've saved 10 cents out of every dollar
I've ever earned since I was 13.

1068
01:49:09,334 --> 01:49:11,461
That's $ 215.

1069
01:49:11,628 --> 01:49:17,425
That's $215 for bludgeting
and the rest.

1070
01:49:20,845 --> 01:49:24,682
This is what I wanted to show you.

1071
01:49:24,849 --> 01:49:27,519
This is where we're gonna go
as soon as you get well.

1072
01:49:28,269 --> 01:49:32,732
San francisco, California.

1073
01:49:35,443 --> 01:49:38,112
You can have anything you want
out there.

1074
01:49:38,279 --> 01:49:44,160
These men are pulling gold right out
of the river with their own hands.

1075
01:49:45,954 --> 01:49:50,291
We're here, and we need to get there.

1076
01:49:51,042 --> 01:49:56,548
We start here, go down around here...

1077
01:49:58,633 --> 01:50:01,261
...to San francisco.

1078
01:50:02,804 --> 01:50:04,848
Shortest way to go.

1079
01:50:05,640 --> 01:50:08,142
Would you go with me?

1080
01:50:32,750 --> 01:50:36,504
No need to fire that, miss.
Or even aim it.

1081
01:50:37,005 --> 01:50:40,425
An audience with your mangled friend
is all I want.

1082
01:50:55,857 --> 01:50:57,942
I got 44 notches in my club.

1083
01:51:00,278 --> 01:51:02,197
Do you know what they're for?

1084
01:51:04,365 --> 01:51:07,285
To remind me of what I owe God
when I die.

1085
01:51:14,292 --> 01:51:17,128
My father was killed in battle too.

1086
01:51:17,795 --> 01:51:20,798
In Ireland, in the streets...

1087
01:51:22,467 --> 01:51:25,220
...fighting those who would take
as their privilege...

1088
01:51:25,386 --> 01:51:29,641
...what could only be got and held
by the decimation of a race.

1089
01:51:30,308 --> 01:51:33,645
That war is 1000 years old or more.

1090
01:51:34,312 --> 01:51:37,232
We never expected it to follow us here.

1091
01:51:39,150 --> 01:51:41,236
It didn't.

1092
01:51:41,402 --> 01:51:44,072
It was waiting for us when we landed.

1093
01:51:47,742 --> 01:51:52,163
Your father tried to carve out a corner
in this country for his tribe.

1094
01:51:53,957 --> 01:51:56,835
That was him.
That was his Dead Rabbits.

1095
01:51:59,754 --> 01:52:03,341
I often wondered,
if he'd lived a bit longer...

1096
01:52:04,175 --> 01:52:06,845
...would he have wanted a bit more?

1097
01:52:18,189 --> 01:52:21,484
Why'd you rifle through
his pockets then?

1098
01:52:38,543 --> 01:52:41,004
For safekeeping.

1099
01:52:43,882 --> 01:52:46,342
I thought maybe you could do with it.

1100
01:53:07,906 --> 01:53:11,034
The blood stays on the blade.

1101
01:54:27,652 --> 01:54:29,821
That's a sorry-looking pelt.

1102
01:54:34,367 --> 01:54:38,371
And it's been so nice and quiet
for the last three months.

1103
01:54:40,540 --> 01:54:44,169
Tell me, this charge,
does it sit uneasy with you?

1104
01:54:45,336 --> 01:54:47,505
No, it's...

1105
01:54:48,381 --> 01:54:50,884
...not uneasy, Bill.

1106
01:54:51,676 --> 01:54:54,220
No, I wouldn't say that.

1107
01:54:56,181 --> 01:55:01,352
But my allegiance is to the law.

1108
01:55:02,395 --> 01:55:05,064
I'm paid to uphold the law.

1109
01:55:20,955 --> 01:55:24,459
What in heaven's name
are you talking about?

1110
01:55:28,546 --> 01:55:31,299
You may have misgivings,
but don't go believing that.

1111
01:55:31,466 --> 01:55:34,135
That way lies damnation.

1112
01:55:37,305 --> 01:55:40,391
I'm in no danger of damnation, Bill.

1113
01:55:51,569 --> 01:55:53,822
Here's the thing.

1114
01:55:54,906 --> 01:55:57,867
I don't give a tuppenny fuck
about your moral conundrum...

1115
01:55:58,034 --> 01:56:00,995
...you meatheaded shit-sack.

1116
01:56:02,330 --> 01:56:05,083
That's more or less the thing.

1117
01:56:06,417 --> 01:56:09,587
And I want you to go out there...
You, nobody else.

1118
01:56:09,754 --> 01:56:14,175
None of your little minions.
I want you to go out there...

1119
01:56:14,342 --> 01:56:16,511
...and I want you...

1120
01:56:17,428 --> 01:56:21,349
...to punish the person...

1121
01:56:21,516 --> 01:56:24,018
...who's responsible...

1122
01:56:26,104 --> 01:56:30,859
...for murdering
this poor little rabbit.

1123
01:56:36,865 --> 01:56:39,033
Is that understood?

1124
01:56:42,871 --> 01:56:44,622
Right.

1125
01:56:45,874 --> 01:56:49,460
Help yourself to some decent meat
on the way out.

1126
01:57:26,623 --> 01:57:28,458
Damn it.

1127
01:57:30,126 --> 01:57:32,170
Is that you, boy?

1128
01:57:33,171 --> 01:57:35,715
I didn't mean nothing
by that shot, you know.

1129
01:57:37,175 --> 01:57:39,969
You scared me is all.

1130
01:57:42,138 --> 01:57:45,016
Sure you know I'd never do you harm.

1131
01:57:45,850 --> 01:57:48,311
Come out now, lad.

1132
01:57:50,522 --> 01:57:53,316
Remember your father and me.

1133
01:57:56,361 --> 01:58:00,156
Are you too young to remember
your old Uncle Jack?

1134
01:58:01,991 --> 01:58:04,661
Oh, the times we had.

1135
01:58:10,208 --> 01:58:12,669
You know I won't hurt you.

1136
01:58:34,274 --> 01:58:36,359
Is he dead?

1137
01:58:36,526 --> 01:58:39,946
I'm gonna get Andrew.
Andrew, come see.

1138
01:58:40,113 --> 01:58:44,367
Andrew, come see, quick!
Everybody, quick!

1139
01:58:44,534 --> 01:58:49,205
This is bad for everybody.
What's next? Dead politicians?

1140
01:58:50,123 --> 01:58:55,295
I could spare half a dozen of you
easier than I can spare him.

1141
01:58:56,045 --> 01:58:59,799
Still, I think it shows dash.

1142
01:58:59,966 --> 01:59:03,970
Give the boy some time.
We'll settle with a good dustup.

1143
01:59:47,347 --> 01:59:50,850
There's more of us coming
off these ships every day.

1144
01:59:51,518 --> 01:59:55,188
I heard 15,000 Irish a week.

1145
01:59:56,189 --> 01:59:58,650
And we're afraid of the Natives.

1146
02:00:00,652 --> 02:00:05,156
Get all of us together,
we ain't got a gang. We got an army.

1147
02:00:05,323 --> 02:00:07,992
And all you nee�s a spark, right?
Just one spark.

1148
02:00:08,159 --> 02:00:10,703
Something to wake us all up.

1149
02:00:27,262 --> 02:00:29,931
It was me played you false.

1150
02:00:31,766 --> 02:00:35,353
- I'd take it back if I could.
- Take it back?

1151
02:00:38,940 --> 02:00:41,526
Johnny, I gotta kill you.

1152
02:00:55,290 --> 02:00:58,543
Get out of the Points
and don't come back.

1153
02:01:06,050 --> 02:01:08,469
Where you going, boyo?

1154
02:01:09,470 --> 02:01:10,972
Come along.

1155
02:01:11,139 --> 02:01:12,974
Come on.

1156
02:01:15,393 --> 02:01:18,313
Tell us, John, what's the trouble?

1157
02:01:19,147 --> 02:01:20,940
No trouble, Bill.

1158
02:01:23,985 --> 02:01:26,613
Still hiding out with your friends?

1159
02:01:29,741 --> 02:01:34,412
No, I'm... I'm not with them, Bill.

1160
02:01:34,579 --> 02:01:37,707
I'm with you. I'm with the Natives.

1161
02:01:42,253 --> 02:01:46,508
You always was a Native
as far as I'm interested, John...

1162
02:01:48,843 --> 02:01:51,763
...till you became a stag.

1163
02:01:55,183 --> 02:01:58,853
You tell me you're a Native.
Do you want to know what a Native is?

1164
02:02:00,688 --> 02:02:04,192
A Native is a man...

1165
02:02:04,359 --> 02:02:09,072
...what's willing to give his life
for his country...

1166
02:02:10,949 --> 02:02:13,284
...like my father done.

1167
02:02:16,371 --> 02:02:20,375
Are you willing to do that,
my young friend?

1168
02:02:29,300 --> 02:02:32,554
John. Johnny!

1169
02:02:40,061 --> 02:02:42,063
Johnny?

1170
02:02:51,489 --> 02:02:53,491
John...

1171
02:03:05,753 --> 02:03:08,590
It hurts too much.

1172
02:03:14,637 --> 02:03:16,806
Just kill me.

1173
02:03:21,352 --> 02:03:23,813
Just kill me.

1174
02:03:25,356 --> 02:03:28,651
Please, please.

1175
02:03:36,159 --> 02:03:38,203
Please.

1176
02:03:40,455 --> 02:03:42,373
Please.

1177
02:03:46,503 --> 02:03:49,005
I'm sorry, Johnny.

1178
02:04:04,395 --> 02:04:06,856
Holy Mary, mother of God,
pray for us sinners...

1179
02:04:07,023 --> 02:04:10,527
...now and at the hour of our death.
Amen.

1180
02:04:11,236 --> 02:04:13,571
Rest in peace, Ma.

1181
02:04:16,282 --> 02:04:18,701
What're you doing here, boyo?

1182
02:04:24,958 --> 02:04:28,294
What's a nigger doing in this church?

1183
02:04:32,549 --> 02:04:34,801
What's a Dead Rabbit doing
with the Natives?

1184
02:04:34,968 --> 02:04:38,137
There's no niggers in the Natives.
Natives and Rabbits is one thing.

1185
02:04:38,304 --> 02:04:40,306
A nigger in the church
is something else!

1186
02:04:40,473 --> 02:04:42,392
You run with the Natives,
pray with them!

1187
02:04:42,559 --> 02:04:45,687
You're gonna wind up on a stake
like your man did!

1188
02:04:46,437 --> 02:04:47,730
McGloin.

1189
02:04:47,897 --> 02:04:52,318
Father! Jesus, did you know
there's a nigger in your church?

1190
02:05:10,336 --> 02:05:12,839
Jesus.

1191
02:05:35,320 --> 02:05:37,864
The earth turns...

1192
02:05:38,656 --> 02:05:41,367
...but we don 't feel it move.

1193
02:05:43,995 --> 02:05:46,831
Then one night you look up.

1194
02:05:47,832 --> 02:05:53,004
One spark, and the sky is on fire.

1195
02:06:04,224 --> 02:06:07,185
Lt's a touching spectacle.

1196
02:06:08,895 --> 02:06:12,023
We'll come back
when you're ready for us.

1197
02:06:18,279 --> 02:06:22,116
The past is the torch
that lights our way.

1198
02:06:22,367 --> 02:06:27,789
Where our fathers have shown us
the path, we shall follow.

1199
02:06:29,624 --> 02:06:33,962
Our faith is the weapon most feared
by our enemies.

1200
02:06:34,212 --> 02:06:39,133
For thereby shall we lift our people up
against those who would destroy us.

1201
02:06:39,300 --> 02:06:44,222
Our name is called the Dead Rabbits
to remind all of our suffering...

1202
02:06:44,389 --> 02:06:48,268
...and as a call to those who suffer still
to join our ranks...

1203
02:06:48,434 --> 02:06:53,231
...however so far they may have strayed
from our common home across the sea.

1204
02:06:53,398 --> 02:06:57,068
For with great numbers
must come great strength...

1205
02:06:58,820 --> 02:07:00,989
...and the salvation of our people.

1206
02:07:01,489 --> 02:07:03,867
- We don't want your business.
- How many men live here?

1207
02:07:04,033 --> 02:07:06,578
- You have to register.
- You can't force me to join.

1208
02:07:06,744 --> 02:07:08,413
- Have you got $300?
- Of course not.

1209
02:07:08,580 --> 02:07:12,041
- Who's got $300?
- Lf you're drafted, release from service...

1210
02:07:12,208 --> 02:07:15,628
...can only be secured for $300,
according to the Conscription Act.

1211
02:07:15,795 --> 02:07:18,423
- Otherwise, you have to serve!
- Who the hell's got $300?

1212
02:07:18,590 --> 02:07:22,260
You have to serve, sir,
unless you have $300!

1213
02:07:22,427 --> 02:07:24,387
- Boys!
- Go back to where you came from!

1214
02:07:24,554 --> 02:07:28,933
I'm born in this country. You immigrated
here. You will fight for this country!

1215
02:07:29,100 --> 02:07:31,769
Sweet Jesus!
War does terrible things to men.

1216
02:07:31,936 --> 02:07:37,942
- Get back uptown where you belong!
- $300? So it's another rich man's war.

1217
02:07:38,109 --> 02:07:41,237
Sir, I request an audience
with this man.

1218
02:07:43,281 --> 02:07:45,450
No one speak English
in New York anymore?

1219
02:07:45,617 --> 02:07:47,744
- Don't understand.
- You do speak English.

1220
02:07:49,662 --> 02:07:54,083
I wonder if Miss Everdeane could
angle her rifle in some other direction.

1221
02:07:59,422 --> 02:08:04,177
I wonder, Mr. Vallon, if you understand
the true value of this sort of publicity.

1222
02:08:08,097 --> 02:08:12,060
The archbishop himself, shoulder to
shoulder with the Irish in the Five Points.

1223
02:08:12,602 --> 02:08:15,772
I'm offering to form an alliance
with you against Bill Cutting...

1224
02:08:15,939 --> 02:08:18,650
...and his slate
of Nativist candidates.

1225
02:08:18,817 --> 02:08:22,821
I'll negotiate a handsome fee for every
Irish vote you send Tammany's way...

1226
02:08:22,987 --> 02:08:25,323
...in the coming elections.

1227
02:08:26,783 --> 02:08:29,953
I need a new friend
in the Five Points, son.

1228
02:08:32,997 --> 02:08:35,458
I'd like that friend to be you.

1229
02:08:38,670 --> 02:08:41,381
Now, just a moment, Mr. Tweed.

1230
02:08:41,548 --> 02:08:44,759
Suppose we do
get you those votes.

1231
02:08:47,095 --> 02:08:49,347
Would you back an Irish candidate
of my choosing?

1232
02:08:49,514 --> 02:08:52,517
- I don't think so.
- What if we get you all the Irish votes?

1233
02:08:52,809 --> 02:08:56,521
That will only happen
in the reign of Queen Dick.

1234
02:08:58,523 --> 02:09:00,733
- Beg your pardon?
- It will never happen.

1235
02:09:00,900 --> 02:09:04,362
I might be persuaded to back an
Irish candidate for, say, alderman.

1236
02:09:04,529 --> 02:09:07,323
- Alderman?
- We've already got Irish aldermen.

1237
02:09:07,490 --> 02:09:10,869
- So we have. That's why...
- Well, what's bigger than alderman?

1238
02:09:12,328 --> 02:09:16,124
Sheriff. Sheriff.
All right, Mr. Tweed.

1239
02:09:16,291 --> 02:09:20,253
You back an Irishman for sheriff
of the city and county of New York...

1240
02:09:20,420 --> 02:09:21,796
...and we'll get him elected.

1241
02:09:21,963 --> 02:09:25,341
I love the Irish, but higher than alderman
you shall never climb.

1242
02:09:25,508 --> 02:09:28,011
- Well, why not?
- For one thing, no man living...

1243
02:09:28,178 --> 02:09:29,971
...can consolidate the Irish vote.
- I can.

1244
02:09:30,138 --> 02:09:32,348
And for another,
I mean no effrontery...

1245
02:09:32,515 --> 02:09:36,769
...no one's yet found an Irish candidate
for sheriff worth voting for.

1246
02:09:39,147 --> 02:09:40,523
Monk.

1247
02:09:41,566 --> 02:09:42,901
Here.

1248
02:09:47,405 --> 02:09:50,074
He's to have me looking as sober
as me own grandfather.

1249
02:09:50,241 --> 02:09:54,412
- Another great man, I'm sure.
- A thorough drunken bastard.

1250
02:09:56,414 --> 02:09:58,208
Could I say what I want?

1251
02:09:59,375 --> 02:10:01,711
Well, that's why I wanted you.

1252
02:10:01,878 --> 02:10:04,797
Our elected representatives
are a gang of thieves...

1253
02:10:04,964 --> 02:10:06,716
...who swear to better our lot...

1254
02:10:06,883 --> 02:10:09,761
...while dipping their hands deep
into our pockets.

1255
02:10:09,928 --> 02:10:13,640
I stand shoulder to shoulder with
community leaders like Bill Cutting...

1256
02:10:14,057 --> 02:10:17,936
...against any and all inroads
into our fine democracy.

1257
02:10:18,102 --> 02:10:22,315
I'll see to it that no one takes away what
you've earned by pluck and application.

1258
02:10:22,482 --> 02:10:24,567
- To invading hordes of Hibernians!

1259
02:10:24,734 --> 02:10:28,780
You go to the polls and you put your
mark next to the name Walter McGinn.

1260
02:10:28,947 --> 02:10:33,618
- Against the potato eaters, like them
over there, thieving our jobs!

1261
02:10:33,785 --> 02:10:36,162
Why should so many
Irish die down south...

1262
02:10:36,329 --> 02:10:40,959
...when the first war to win is not down
in Dixie, but right here in these streets?

1263
02:10:42,794 --> 02:10:45,588
And who's the finest street fighter
in the Five Points?

1264
02:10:45,797 --> 02:10:47,966
- Monk!
- That's right.

1265
02:10:48,132 --> 02:10:50,885
Now, let the whole damn city hear it!

1266
02:10:52,303 --> 02:10:54,138
That man was right born for this.

1267
02:10:54,305 --> 02:10:57,350
Well, he's killed 44 men,
laid low a couple hundred more.

1268
02:10:57,517 --> 02:10:59,185
- Is that right?
- That's right.

1269
02:10:59,352 --> 02:11:01,437
I should have run him for mayor.

1270
02:11:03,648 --> 02:11:06,943
All right, line them up.
Lt's election day!

1271
02:11:07,443 --> 02:11:10,780
Not so fast,
you little yellow bastard!

1272
02:11:10,989 --> 02:11:12,824
Come on!

1273
02:11:16,536 --> 02:11:18,538
Come on, you bastard,
we need your vote.

1274
02:11:18,705 --> 02:11:22,125
Bastard? I fought for you, nigger.
I lost an arm for you.

1275
02:11:22,292 --> 02:11:24,127
Well, that's a start.
Now, come on.

1276
02:11:24,294 --> 02:11:26,045
Rise and shine.
Rise and shine.

1277
02:11:26,212 --> 02:11:29,382
In this great country of ours,
even the hop fiends get to vote!

1278
02:11:30,675 --> 02:11:32,510
Less art and more haste, Don.

1279
02:11:32,677 --> 02:11:35,180
- Where are you going?
- I already voted today.

1280
02:11:35,346 --> 02:11:38,349
Cast for Monk and Tammany,
by God. Twice.

1281
02:11:38,516 --> 02:11:42,353
Twice? Only twice? You call that doing
your civic duty? Come with me.

1282
02:11:42,520 --> 02:11:45,148
Oh, no, you don't.
Get back and sit down.

1283
02:11:45,315 --> 02:11:47,400
Here's another one.
Clean him up good.

1284
02:11:47,567 --> 02:11:50,236
Shave their beards and send them back
to vote again.

1285
02:11:50,403 --> 02:11:53,281
All right, boys. Vote Monk, Tammany.
Monk, Tammany!

1286
02:11:53,448 --> 02:11:56,534
So, gentlemen, what our great city
needs is a new courthouse.

1287
02:11:56,701 --> 02:11:59,579
I propose it should be a modest,
economical structure.

1288
02:11:59,746 --> 02:12:01,539
Excuse me one moment.

1289
02:12:01,706 --> 02:12:05,251
Monk's already won by 3000
more votes than there are voters.

1290
02:12:05,418 --> 02:12:08,963
Three? Make it 20, 30. We don't need
a victory. We need a Roman triumph!

1291
02:12:09,130 --> 02:12:13,384
- But we don't have any more ballots.
- Remember the first rule of politics:

1292
02:12:13,551 --> 02:12:16,930
The ballots don't make the results,
the counters make the results.

1293
02:12:17,096 --> 02:12:18,223
Keep counting.

1294
02:12:50,088 --> 02:12:52,090
Lt's from Bill.

1295
02:12:55,510 --> 02:12:57,095
Monk.

1296
02:13:16,823 --> 02:13:19,826
Citizens of the Five Points...

1297
02:13:20,326 --> 02:13:24,247
...Mr. Bill Cutting is attempting
to draw me into an argument...

1298
02:13:24,414 --> 02:13:29,002
...that would no doubt end in bloodshed
and the compromising of my office.

1299
02:13:29,669 --> 02:13:31,629
What do you think?

1300
02:13:32,547 --> 02:13:36,217
Should I engage in silence
this relic of the ancient law?

1301
02:13:36,384 --> 02:13:42,807
Or shall I be your chosen voice
in a new testament, in the new world?

1302
02:13:48,771 --> 02:13:50,565
There you are, Bill.

1303
02:13:52,192 --> 02:13:54,194
The people have spoken.

1304
02:13:55,695 --> 02:13:59,782
The very notion of violent reprisal
benumbs them.

1305
02:14:02,035 --> 02:14:03,661
Come on up.

1306
02:14:04,245 --> 02:14:07,665
Let's see if we can resolve
our grievances the democratic way.

1307
02:14:30,939 --> 02:14:35,735
That, my friends,
is the minority vote.

1308
02:14:36,778 --> 02:14:40,824
Now you've tasted my mutton.
How do you like it?

1309
02:14:40,990 --> 02:14:42,367
Look.

1310
02:14:42,534 --> 02:14:45,078
I want you to see this.

1311
02:14:47,914 --> 02:14:50,041
This is you. Right here.

1312
02:14:50,208 --> 02:14:54,087
Notch 45, you Irish bog bastard.

1313
02:15:05,557 --> 02:15:09,060
Why don't you burn him,
see if his ashes turn green.

1314
02:15:14,357 --> 02:15:16,734
Fortune favors the bold!

1315
02:15:29,164 --> 02:15:33,251
- You killed an elected official?
- Who elected him?

1316
02:15:33,418 --> 02:15:36,588
You don't know what
you've done to yourself.

1317
02:15:42,093 --> 02:15:44,637
I know your works.

1318
02:15:45,263 --> 02:15:47,473
You are neither cold nor hot.

1319
02:15:47,640 --> 02:15:51,936
So because you are lukewarm,
I will spew you out of my mouth.

1320
02:15:53,521 --> 02:15:56,024
You can build your filthy world
without me.

1321
02:15:56,191 --> 02:15:58,651
I took the father.
Now I'll take the son.

1322
02:15:58,818 --> 02:16:03,740
You tell young Vallon I'm gonna
paint Paradise Square with his blood.

1323
02:16:03,990 --> 02:16:05,575
Two coats.

1324
02:16:06,159 --> 02:16:08,828
I'll festoon my bedchamber
with his guts.

1325
02:16:08,995 --> 02:16:11,456
As for you,
Mr. Tammany-fucking-Hall...

1326
02:16:11,623 --> 02:16:13,541
...you come down
to the Points again...

1327
02:16:13,708 --> 02:16:17,212
...and you'll be dispatched
by mine own hand.

1328
02:16:19,005 --> 02:16:24,385
Now get back to your celebration
and let me eat in peace. I paid you fair.

1329
02:16:48,201 --> 02:16:49,911
Stop.

1330
02:16:50,912 --> 02:16:52,497
Raise.

1331
02:17:35,582 --> 02:17:37,834
You son of a bitch!

1332
02:17:41,671 --> 02:17:43,673
Challenge.

1333
02:17:45,049 --> 02:17:47,427
Challenge accepted.

1334
02:18:39,103 --> 02:18:42,524
And then it came,
the first day of the draft.

1335
02:18:42,690 --> 02:18:48,071
Thomas O'Neill. Andrew Lewis.
Joseph flynn.

1336
02:18:48,238 --> 02:18:51,449
They read out the draftees ' names
like they was dead already.

1337
02:18:51,616 --> 02:18:55,954
Andrew Martin. J.B. Gleason.

1338
02:18:56,120 --> 02:18:58,873
- The O'Connell Guard.
- The Plug Uglies.

1339
02:18:59,040 --> 02:19:02,293
- As for us, the tribes were gathered.
- Forty Thieves.

1340
02:19:02,627 --> 02:19:05,129
- The Chichesters.
- The drums was beating.

1341
02:19:05,296 --> 02:19:06,631
The Dead Rabbits.

1342
02:19:06,798 --> 02:19:09,759
- The American Guard.
- The Atlantic Guard.

1343
02:19:09,926 --> 02:19:12,595
- The Slaughter Housers.
- The Bowery Boys.

1344
02:19:12,762 --> 02:19:15,098
The Confederation
of American Natives.

1345
02:19:16,516 --> 02:19:17,642
James Mooney.

1346
02:19:17,809 --> 02:19:21,312
But all we could see
was the hand in front of us.

1347
02:19:21,479 --> 02:19:23,481
Sean O'Connell.

1348
02:19:23,648 --> 02:19:27,819
G.W. Morris. John Doyle.

1349
02:19:27,986 --> 02:19:30,655
- When?
- Whenever you like.

1350
02:19:30,822 --> 02:19:33,032
- Daybreak tomorrow.
- Ground?

1351
02:19:33,199 --> 02:19:35,326
Paradise Square.

1352
02:19:35,493 --> 02:19:37,287
Weapons?

1353
02:19:37,453 --> 02:19:42,709
- That I leave up to you.
- Bricks, bats, axes, knives...

1354
02:19:43,042 --> 02:19:44,502
Pistols?

1355
02:19:46,045 --> 02:19:47,505
No pistols.

1356
02:19:48,631 --> 02:19:50,675
Good boy.

1357
02:19:53,219 --> 02:19:56,264
The terms are resolved.
The council's concluded.

1358
02:19:58,183 --> 02:20:02,228
Thomas McEvoy. W.H. Miles.

1359
02:20:02,395 --> 02:20:05,482
- To hell with your damned draft!
- Give them hellfire, boys!

1360
02:20:05,648 --> 02:20:07,192
Rip them apart!

1361
02:20:14,240 --> 02:20:16,784
Kill the rich bastards!

1362
02:20:22,999 --> 02:20:24,626
Uptown!

1363
02:20:26,211 --> 02:20:28,004
You're not getting my son!

1364
02:20:33,551 --> 02:20:36,221
Uptown at the draft office...

1365
02:20:36,387 --> 02:20:41,434
...the cops broke a few skulls,
and everybody run away.

1366
02:20:42,435 --> 02:20:44,854
But the word was spreading...

1367
02:20:45,021 --> 02:20:46,898
...and the dark.

1368
02:20:48,066 --> 02:20:49,442
Up on Fifth Avenue...

1369
02:20:49,609 --> 02:20:52,946
...the Schermerhorns and their mob
breathed a little easy.

1370
02:20:53,112 --> 02:20:56,115
Just a brief burst of anger
over Mr. Lincoln's draft.

1371
02:20:56,282 --> 02:20:58,535
Entirely justifiable in my view.

1372
02:20:58,701 --> 02:21:02,413
- There are two sides to that question...
- There are several sides...

1373
02:21:02,580 --> 02:21:05,124
...but only one right side,
as with any question...

1374
02:21:05,291 --> 02:21:07,585
...that involves the abuse
of executive power.

1375
02:21:07,752 --> 02:21:10,088
In any case, Mr. Greeley...

1376
02:21:10,255 --> 02:21:12,966
...we can all be thankful
that it wasn't any worse.

1377
02:21:13,132 --> 02:21:16,261
It may be worse yet. I saw them.
I don't know what to think.

1378
02:21:16,427 --> 02:21:19,639
Now what is it that you are
so fond of saying, Mr. Tweed?

1379
02:21:19,806 --> 02:21:22,267
Mr. Greeley, you won't like this.
But what is it?

1380
02:21:22,433 --> 02:21:23,476
I don't remember.

1381
02:21:23,643 --> 02:21:28,398
"You can always hire one half
of the poor to kill the other half."

1382
02:21:38,658 --> 02:21:40,994
I've come to say goodbye.

1383
02:21:41,161 --> 02:21:44,122
I've booked passage for California.

1384
02:21:46,624 --> 02:21:49,419
Jenny, give me one more day,
and I'll go with you.

1385
02:21:49,586 --> 02:21:51,921
You'll be dead by then.

1386
02:21:52,463 --> 02:21:55,925
- Well, what would you have me do?
- I don't know.

1387
02:21:58,553 --> 02:22:02,307
- This will all be finished tomorrow.
- No, it won't.

1388
02:22:03,933 --> 02:22:05,476
No.

1389
02:22:05,894 --> 02:22:09,022
This whole place
is gonna burn anyway.

1390
02:22:17,071 --> 02:22:21,826
I've heard that the poor are going
from door to door in the Five Points...

1391
02:22:21,993 --> 02:22:25,538
...asking those supporters
who wish to see further riots...

1392
02:22:25,705 --> 02:22:28,833
...to place a lighted candle
in the window.

1393
02:22:29,250 --> 02:22:31,336
Irish, Poles, Germans, all of them.

1394
02:22:32,128 --> 02:22:38,343
Mr. Greeley, the city is not mad.
I prophesy a very dark night.

1395
02:23:00,573 --> 02:23:04,410
Nobody goes to work today!
We'll shut the factories down!

1396
02:23:04,577 --> 02:23:08,998
When the sun rose next,
the city had split in half.

1397
02:23:09,165 --> 02:23:11,000
From all over New York they came:

1398
02:23:11,167 --> 02:23:14,003
Ironworkers, factory boys,
street cleaners...

1399
02:23:14,170 --> 02:23:15,755
...Irish, Polish, German...

1400
02:23:15,922 --> 02:23:18,716
...anyone who never cared
about slavery or the Union...

1401
02:23:18,883 --> 02:23:21,469
...anyone who couldn 't buy his way out.

1402
02:23:21,636 --> 02:23:24,639
"Let the sons of the rich
go and die, " they cried.

1403
02:23:24,806 --> 02:23:27,851
"Let the sons of the poor stay home."

1404
02:23:29,227 --> 02:23:31,396
The earth was shaking now.

1405
02:23:31,563 --> 02:23:34,274
But I was about my father's business.

1406
02:23:39,362 --> 02:23:41,573
A bunch of fifth Avenue scums!

1407
02:23:48,746 --> 02:23:53,251
Almighty Lord,
you are the dagger in my hand.

1408
02:23:54,544 --> 02:23:56,921
Guide my hand
on this day of vengeance.

1409
02:23:57,088 --> 02:24:01,092
We give thanks to the Lord,
for he is good.

1410
02:24:01,259 --> 02:24:05,597
With you, the swift cannot flee,
nor the strong escape.

1411
02:24:05,763 --> 02:24:09,934
Let my sword devour until its thirst
is quenched with blood...

1412
02:24:10,101 --> 02:24:12,103
...and my enemies sleep forever.

1413
02:24:12,270 --> 02:24:14,647
For you are the Lord God of Retribution.

1414
02:24:14,814 --> 02:24:17,275
For the Lord crushes the wicked.

1415
02:24:17,442 --> 02:24:20,069
The Lord is merciful...

1416
02:24:20,695 --> 02:24:24,491
...and his love endures forever.

1417
02:24:25,033 --> 02:24:26,409
- Amen.
- Amen.

1418
02:24:26,576 --> 02:24:27,744
Amen.

1419
02:24:33,750 --> 02:24:36,794
- Run away, you sons of bitches!
- Come on out!

1420
02:24:39,339 --> 02:24:43,468
- Upstairs! Get the women upstairs!
- Father! Father!

1421
02:24:47,305 --> 02:24:49,807
Let's take the bastards!

1422
02:24:50,808 --> 02:24:54,813
From 18th Precinct: The mob are sacking
houses, 27th Street and Seventh Ave.

1423
02:24:54,979 --> 02:24:56,356
We have no force to send.

1424
02:24:56,523 --> 02:24:59,818
16th Precinct: All the stores
are closing on Eighth Avenue...

1425
02:24:59,984 --> 02:25:01,986
...from fear of the mob in 17th Street.

1426
02:25:02,153 --> 02:25:04,656
Show us what you got,
you cowardly bastards!

1427
02:25:06,449 --> 02:25:09,536
From 4th: The rioters are robbing
colored boarding houses...

1428
02:25:09,702 --> 02:25:12,163
...and setting them on fire.
From 21st:

1429
02:25:13,540 --> 02:25:16,501
The mob have just broken open
gun store and are arming.

1430
02:25:21,172 --> 02:25:23,716
- Get the nigger! Kill him!
- Tear Tammany down!

1431
02:25:23,883 --> 02:25:27,095
The marshal's office is burning down.
The police is of no avail.

1432
02:25:27,262 --> 02:25:28,346
Show yourself, Tweed!

1433
02:25:28,513 --> 02:25:30,223
Two colored men brought
in almost dead.

1434
02:25:32,141 --> 02:25:35,687
There are more Negroes who need help.
They're being attacked all over.

1435
02:25:35,854 --> 02:25:37,647
Hey! There's a $300 man.
Get him!

1436
02:25:37,814 --> 02:25:40,942
Did your daddy buy you out of the Army?
Can he buy me out too?

1437
02:25:42,318 --> 02:25:46,364
There's danger of mob attacking armory,
corner 21st Street, Second Avenue.

1438
02:25:46,531 --> 02:25:48,741
There is about 500 stand of arms in it.

1439
02:25:54,789 --> 02:25:57,876
The line is dead!
They're trying to cut all the wires!

1440
02:26:01,171 --> 02:26:04,591
From 1st: Riot at Pier 4, North River.
They have killed Negroes.

1441
02:26:04,757 --> 02:26:07,760
A crowd is here
and are going to destroy this station.

1442
02:26:10,722 --> 02:26:12,932
Get her bag! Get her bag!

1443
02:26:15,059 --> 02:26:16,102
From 18th Precinct:

1444
02:26:16,269 --> 02:26:21,024
The mob have attacked the armory.
There is danger of firing the building.

1445
02:26:21,191 --> 02:26:23,735
All 300 police wounded
or unaccounted for.

1446
02:26:23,902 --> 02:26:25,445
Find the military
and send them...

1447
02:26:25,612 --> 02:26:29,949
From 20th: Send 100 men to disperse
mob assailing Mayor Opdyke's house.

1448
02:26:30,116 --> 02:26:31,868
Building set on fire by the mob.

1449
02:26:34,704 --> 02:26:38,082
Barnum 's American Muzeum on fire.
Animals are escaping.

1450
02:26:38,249 --> 02:26:40,710
Let go! Let go!

1451
02:26:41,294 --> 02:26:43,922
Hush the shickster! Hold her down!

1452
02:26:55,642 --> 02:26:59,646
Gunboat Liberty and ironclad Passaic
now lying off the foot of Wall Street.

1453
02:26:59,813 --> 02:27:01,815
They are ready to open fire on the mob.

1454
02:27:01,981 --> 02:27:04,400
- Very large mob going
to attack Tribune office.

1455
02:27:05,819 --> 02:27:08,488
From 16th: Mob 's coming down
to station. We have no men.

1456
02:27:08,655 --> 02:27:11,991
- 18th: The mob is very wild.
- From 16th: 7th Regiment has arrived.

1457
02:27:12,158 --> 02:27:14,285
Foot of Canal, they are
on their way to Broadway.

1458
02:27:14,452 --> 02:27:15,703
Take your hands off me!

1459
02:27:15,870 --> 02:27:17,872
The mob is about 4500 strong.

1460
02:27:18,039 --> 02:27:21,125
They're going to burn down
Harlem Bridge. It is to be torched.

1461
02:27:21,292 --> 02:27:24,254
- Mr. Greeley!
- The rats have taken over the city!

1462
02:27:27,423 --> 02:27:31,219
From 21st: There is an attack on the
colored people in Second Avenue...

1463
02:27:31,386 --> 02:27:34,597
Mob headed for the Colored Orphans
Asylum. Protect the children!

1464
02:27:34,764 --> 02:27:36,516
Rioters on Seventh Avenue,
28th Street.

1465
02:27:37,934 --> 02:27:39,769
- They have killed a Negro.
- Get him!

1466
02:27:39,936 --> 02:27:42,147
What do we do with captured prisoners?

1467
02:27:42,313 --> 02:27:45,233
Prisoners? Don't take any.
The mob isn't taking prisoners!

1468
02:27:45,400 --> 02:27:48,862
Don't take a prisoner in
until you've put the mob down!

1469
02:27:56,161 --> 02:28:00,165
Soldiers now on 38th Street.
The mob will not disperse.

1470
02:28:00,331 --> 02:28:02,834
What are your orders?
What are your orders?

1471
02:28:17,557 --> 02:28:22,520
- No draft! No draft!
- Show them what we think of their draft!

1472
02:28:29,319 --> 02:28:30,945
- Stick together!
- All right.

1473
02:28:35,074 --> 02:28:37,202
Troop, halt!

1474
02:28:37,869 --> 02:28:40,371
Present arms!

1475
02:28:46,252 --> 02:28:47,754
On your order, Vallon.

1476
02:28:50,006 --> 02:28:51,841
Hold fast, there.
Hold fast, there.

1477
02:28:52,008 --> 02:28:54,844
I order you to disperse!

1478
02:28:58,097 --> 02:28:59,682
Fire!

1479
02:29:35,093 --> 02:29:37,387
Fire starboard!

1480
02:29:50,567 --> 02:29:53,403
Starboard battery, fire!

1481
02:30:02,996 --> 02:30:04,622
Come on, let's go!

1482
02:30:10,962 --> 02:30:13,131
Fire over their heads!

1483
02:30:13,965 --> 02:30:16,134
Drive them into the square!

1484
02:30:18,887 --> 02:30:20,680
Bill!

1485
02:30:28,271 --> 02:30:29,647
Fire!

1486
02:30:30,315 --> 02:30:31,816
Advance!

1487
02:30:32,567 --> 02:30:34,486
Bill!

1488
02:30:37,572 --> 02:30:38,990
I can't walk!

1489
02:30:40,783 --> 02:30:42,327
Get up!

1490
02:31:45,890 --> 02:31:47,267
Fire!

1491
02:32:15,587 --> 02:32:19,424
- Bastards!
- Shang! Shang, hold off!

1492
02:32:51,789 --> 02:32:53,917
Thank God.

1493
02:32:54,876 --> 02:32:57,712
I die a true American.

1494
02:34:59,709 --> 02:35:02,253
Tomorrow morning,
get our people down to the docks.

1495
02:35:03,338 --> 02:35:07,884
I want every man and woman coming off
the boats given hot soup and bread.

1496
02:35:12,388 --> 02:35:15,892
We're burying a lot of votes
down here tonight.

1497
02:35:33,409 --> 02:35:38,248
In the end, they put candles
on the bodies so 's their friends...

1498
02:35:38,414 --> 02:35:42,627
...if they had any,
could know them in the dark.

1499
02:35:43,795 --> 02:35:47,257
The city did this free of charge.

1500
02:35:48,299 --> 02:35:54,347
Shang, Jimmy Spoils,
Hell-Cat, McGloin and more.

1501
02:35:55,849 --> 02:36:00,311
Friend or foe,
it didn 't make no difference now.

1502
02:36:19,831 --> 02:36:25,837
It was four days and nights before the
worst of the mob was finally put down.

1503
02:36:26,880 --> 02:36:29,549
We never knew how many
New Yorkers died that week...

1504
02:36:29,716 --> 02:36:32,510
...before the city was finally delivered.

1505
02:36:33,887 --> 02:36:38,975
My father told me we was all born
of blood and tribulation.

1506
02:36:40,101 --> 02:36:44,397
And so then too was our great city.

1507
02:36:47,317 --> 02:36:52,071
But for those of us what lived
and died in them furious days...

1508
02:36:52,238 --> 02:36:57,702
...it was like everything we knew
was mightily swept away.

1509
02:36:58,495 --> 02:37:01,623
And no matter what they did
to build the city up again...

1510
02:37:03,875 --> 02:37:06,544
...for the rest of time...

1511
02:37:07,504 --> 02:37:11,799
...it would be like no one even knew
we was ever here.

1512
02:46:31,401 --> 02:46:32,443
Subtitles by
SDI Media Group

1513
02:46:32,610 --> 02:46:33,653
[ENGLISH]


